export const PAGES: Record<string, CodexPage> = {"story/README": {"title": "The Long Noon", "sub": "The story of the Dial, from the making of the Tower to the new sun: the whole arc in a page, and the fourteen chapters.", "blocks": [["p", "**Where we are.** Chapters 1 to 6, and chapter 7 up to its signs, are the Dial's history, up to now: the spring of the hundredth summer, where the rest of the Codex stands. From the night the Uncrowned walks into the Truce Table, the Story tells what is still to come."], ["h", 2, "The story in a page"], ["p", "Long ago, on a world called Senmere whose sun was going out, a keeper of vaults called Iselm could not bear to lose his dying daughter, and built a thing that could keep her: a tower of seamless stone that takes a thing at its hour and holds it, as it was, for ever. He cut its word at its root, and the word was his own name, which meant *keep*. It kept his daughter, and then his people's sun, and then their whole world, at noon, on its floors. Then, with nothing left to keep and the word still binding it, it crossed the dark between the stars to the next world with an ageing sun, dragging the evening that world was never allowed to have behind it. Iselm went with it, as its keeper."], ["p", "That world was the Dial. The Tower, the **Aumbry**, stood at its centre before anyone remembered, and its shadow became the Dial's clock while it learned every realm. Four hundred years before the Fall its keeper, the Sun Warden, taught Aubade a hymn to sing at its top each noon; the Hymn drew the sun to the Tower, and only its last verse, sung by the choir's master at midsummer, made it let go. At noon on midsummer of year 0 the Tower closed its hand. It took the choir in the hour of noon, the master first, before the last verse could be sung, and took the sun's noon; the sun's body cracked in its grip and fell. One singer, a hair behind the beat, was left: Mum, who knew the last verse, and has not made a sound since. The Tower took the realms' worst hours one by one, the drowned harbour, the walking Wood, the storm, the dark halls, the war's last night; the realms went to war over the sun's pieces, and then swore the Truce of the Ring in the Tower's shadow, which the Tower took as its own law."], ["p", "For ninety years its gate stayed shut. In year 90 Mum won a duel in the Ring, and the Tower, unable to take her, took the hour of her win (the Tally's blank page) and opened its gate to lure her in; the Dial began to climb. In the hundredth summer the exiled queen who had read the truth on a tomb wall tells the Truce Table what the Tower is, and that it means to take everything at noon on the Hundredth Midsummer and leave. The Truce sends a company up the Climb, and its officers hold the Ring. The company frees one kept hour after another, meets the Tower's keeper, and learns that a made thing obeys the last word written on it. At noon on the Hundredth Midsummer Mum sings the last verse, a fox spirit steals the Tower's word out of its keeper's mouth, a showman makes it vanish in his hat, a courier runs a new word in a calligrapher's hand a mile down to the Tower's root, and a golem-maker cuts it there: *remember*. Below, a knight sets the last light of the old sun into the fallen Heart; above, the Heart's light, the sun's two last sparks and the Firebird meet the kept noon, and a new sun rises over the Tower. Senmere has its evening at last, the Unmaker goes home into it and ends, the Tear is sewn shut, and the Mountain steps out of the Ring, and the shadow moves."], ["p", "The Tower still stands, asleep, remembering instead of keeping, and is letting its keepings go one at a time. Three realms and older hours are still inside it, something came down through the Tear before it closed, and a red star is coming."], ["h", 2, "The chapters"], ["p", "**Part one: before the Dial**"], ["ul", ["[1. The Aumbry](story/01-the-aumbry): Senmere, Iselm and his daughter, the making of the Tower and its word, the keeping of a world, the crossing.", "[2. The Gnomon](story/02-the-gnomon): the Dial grows up in the Tower's shadow; the first king's tomb; the Hymn; the paling; the Uncrowned opens the tomb; the last week."]], ["p", "**Part two: the Fall**"], ["ul", ["[3. Midsummer](story/03-midsummer): the day of the Fall, hour by hour, at the top, at the foot and across the Dial.", "[4. The Banners](story/04-the-banners): years 0 to 2; the Tower takes its first realms; the night before the Ninth Field; the Truce sworn in its shadow."]], ["p", "**Part three: the century**"], ["ul", ["[5. The Hundred Years](story/05-the-hundred-years): the Hundred Doors, the Settling, the man who came out of a door, the first great generation.", "[6. The Gate](story/06-the-gate): the duel of year 90, the gate, the Climb's five chapters, Noonfall and the Raid, the Trials, the door under the sea temple.", "[7. The Hundredth Summer](story/07-the-hundredth-summer): now; the Uncrowned at the Truce Table; the blank page written; the council; the company."]], ["p", "**Part four: the Climb**"], ["ul", ["[8. The Sea and the Wood](story/08-the-sea-and-the-wood): floors 1 to 20.", "[9. The Sands and the Deep](story/09-the-sands-and-the-deep): floors 21 to 40, and the Tower's keeper shows what it is.", "[10. The War](story/10-the-war): floors 41 to 50, and the first kept hour to end."]], ["p", "**Part five: the top**"], ["ul", ["[11. Above the Fiftieth](story/11-above-the-fiftieth): the three realms never shown, the Sun Court, the Noon Seat, the window onto Senmere.", "[12. The Last Verse](story/12-the-last-verse): noon on the Hundredth Midsummer.", "[13. After](story/13-after): what came out of the Tower, and what each of them got.", "[14. The Floors Above](story/14-the-floors-above): epilogue; the keepings not yet opened, what came through the Tear, the red star."]], ["h", 2, "Where the modes are in the story"], ["ul", ["**The duels** are fought in the Ring, on the Tower's threshold, under the Truce the Tower keeps; the Tower watches every one and keeps an echo of each champion ([chapter 5](story/05-the-hundred-years)).", "**The Climb** is the Tower's collection: five realms taken at their worst hours, their people, their rulers, and its keepings of the sun's pieces as its crowns ([chapters 6](story/06-the-gate), [8](story/08-the-sea-and-the-wood), [9](story/09-the-sands-and-the-deep), [10](story/10-the-war)). After the ending it is the Tower's memory of those hours, and is still climbed ([chapter 14](story/14-the-floors-above)).", "**The Raid** is fought in Noonfall, the hall above the Heart, against the sun's pieces themselves, which the Tower bound to guard the Heart it drank: the Warden's sentinel, Ash and Ember, the Heart of Noon. Each win brightened the next morning ([chapter 6](story/06-the-gate)); the last Raid carried the Ember down ([chapter 12](story/12-the-last-verse)).", "**The Trials**, the weekly Tower, are the Tower measuring the Dial's champions with its echoes of them ([chapter 6](story/06-the-gate))."]], ["h", 2, "Where the heroes come from"], ["p", "Most heroes are Dial-born. Six are not: **Returned** (taken by the Tower long ago and come back out), **Half-kept** (half taken in the hour of the Fall), **Spilled** (made by the Fall's broken taking) and **Followed** (came after the Tower through the Tear). The heroes whose trades seem odd for a world at war (a referee, a cook, a courier, a herdsman) are odd because of the Ring, not the Tower: the Truce made its officers out of whoever was standing at the Tower's foot when the war ended."], ["t", ["Hero", "From", "How"], [["[The Great Zambini](heroes/the-great-zambini)", "**Returned**", "Taken with a Fox court revel three hundred years before the Fall; walked out of a Hundred Door in year 40"], ["[Tempo](heroes/tempo)", "**Half-kept**", "A heartbeat behind the hour when the Tower closed its hand: one of him was kept, one walks"], ["[Anka](heroes/anka)", "**Spilled**", "A spark of the sun that slipped the Tower's grip and fell into the sea"], ["[Nyx](heroes/nyx)", "**Spilled**", "A shadow thrown by light the Tower took in mid-throw, left belonging to nothing"], ["[Oblivion](heroes/oblivion)", "**Followed**", "The ending of Senmere, the world the Tower kept before the Dial, dragged after it through the Tear"], ["[Ada](heroes/ada)", "Dial-born", "Born in the Deep in year 74; went to look at the Tower's gears and found a word"], ["[Auriel](heroes/auriel)", "Dial-born", "Born in Aubade the morning after the Fall, daughter of a tenor the Tower kept"], ["[Barry Cloudsley](heroes/barry-cloudsley)", "Dial-born", "Born on the Coast; saw the Tower taking the weather into its floors"], ["[Big Yoko](heroes/big-yoko)", "Dial-born", "Born on the shrine cliffs below Aubade; his circle stands where the Tower's shadow stopped, and holds it"], ["[Bo](heroes/bo)", "Dial-born", "Born on the high pastures; his sheep turned to the Tower for nine heartbeats every midsummer"], ["[Bram](heroes/bram)", "Dial-born", "Born in the Deep; his friend Gerald is one of the Tower's nine warning stones"], ["[Brannoc](heroes/brannoc)", "Dial-born", "Born in the hills before they went grey; his coal burns low as the Tower drinks the Heart"], ["[Cantor](heroes/cantor)", "Dial-born", "Born in Aubade; left in the Sun Court because he was listening to Mum and not the beat"], ["[Fenna](heroes/fenna)", "Dial-born", "Born in the Wood; the Tower drew the oak she planted into its Wood"], ["[Grandma Ironknit](heroes/grandma-ironknit)", "Dial-born", "A Briarkin of the Spindle court, at the Wood's edge; dropped three stitches at the Fall"], ["[Gus](heroes/gus)", "Dial-born", "Born in Saltreach in year 35; swept the Tower's drowned temple clean"], ["[Hank](heroes/hank)", "Dial-born", "Born at the Wood's edge; cut the Tower's root, and the cut would not heal"], ["[Isolde](heroes/isolde)", "Dial-born", "Born at Frostfall Lake; held the cold, so the Tower passed her by in the Long Hold"], ["[Kaia](heroes/kaia)", "Dial-born", "Born on the Spires in year 72; her storms always went round the Tower"], ["[Lyra](heroes/lyra)", "Dial-born", "Born at Frostfall in year 78; her red star is coming"], ["[Mabel](heroes/mabel)", "Dial-born", "Born in the deepest seams; found a tunnel dug from below, a root of the Tower"], ["[Madame Cleaver](heroes/madame-cleaver)", "Dial-born", "Born in the Sands, a caravan cook's daughter; her fire ends the war the Tower kept"], ["[Marin](heroes/marin)", "Dial-born", "Born on the Drowned Coast; danced the water out, so the Tower passed her by"], ["[Mortis](heroes/mortis)", "Dial-born", "Born in Gloaming, dead three hundred years; the Tower's keeping is why the dead get up"], ["[Mr. Ledger](heroes/mr-ledger)", "Dial-born", "Born in Harrow, dead since sixty years before the Fall; the Tower took and never paid, and he writes the bill"], ["[Mum](heroes/mum)", "Dial-born", "Born in Aubade; a hair behind the beat since the Fall, so the Tower cannot take her by an hour"], ["[Opaline](heroes/opaline)", "Dial-born", "Born in Gloaming's mirror quarter; holds the colour the sun lost"], ["[Pim and Pom](heroes/pim-and-pom)", "Dial-born", "Born in the Underbough, one life for two, as the Tower's grip on the noon split some Briarkin lives"], ["[Pip](heroes/pip)", "Dial-born", "Born in Aubade in year 81; carries the Truce's challenge to the Tower and the new word down it"], ["[Professor Puff](heroes/professor-puff)", "Dial-born", "Born on the Coast; felt the Tower breathe in at the Fall"], ["[Referee Whistler](heroes/referee-whistler)", "Dial-born", "Born in the Deep's masons' halls; sweeping the straw circle when the Tower took the market and his father with it"], ["[Sefu](heroes/sefu)", "Dial-born", "Born in Tamerit; learns at the thirtieth floor that the Father was taken"], ["[Shiori](heroes/shiori)", "Dial-born", "Born at Frostfall in year 70; writes the new word"], ["[Silvane](heroes/silvane)", "Dial-born", "Born in Gloaming's mirror quarter; caught the sun's last look in a mirror"], ["[Sir Whiskers](heroes/sir-whiskers)", "Dial-born", "Born at the Keep of Nine; his brother was kept on the War floors"], ["[Sirix](heroes/sirix)", "Dial-born", "Born in the Wood; his swarm came up out of the Tower's roots"], ["[Vesper](heroes/vesper)", "Dial-born", "Born in Gloaming, kept since by blood, not by the Tower; the Long Dusk she ruled is one of its keepings"], ["[Vitriol](heroes/vitriol)", "Dial-born", "Born in Tamerit, kept since by the embalmers' oil"], ["[Wick](heroes/wick)", "Dial-born", "Born at Frostfall Lake; keeps the Tower's own doors, which it cannot use while he keeps them"], ["[Zephyr](heroes/zephyr)", "Dial-born", "Born on the Spires in year 76; jumped from the Noon Seat"]]]]}, "story/01-the-aumbry": {"title": "1. The Aumbry", "sub": "Part one, before the Dial. A world under a failing sun, a man who could not bear to lose his daughter, and the thing he made.", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Senmere"], ["p", "Long before the Dial had a name, on a world so far from it that its sun is not one of the Dial's stars, there was a country of terraces called **Senmere**. Its people built on the sides of a great round valley, tier above tier, white stone and blue glass, so that every house had a view of the sky. They loved the sky more than anything they owned. Their sun was large and gold and slow, and when it stood over the valley at noon the whole country fell quiet for a moment, every year of their history, because that was the custom: at noon you stopped and looked up, and said nothing, and were glad."], ["p", "They were a careful people. They wrote everything down and kept everything they could. Under every terrace were vaults where the families kept what they did not want to lose: letters, shoes, the first drawing a child had made, a lock of a grandmother's hair. A family's vault was the most private thing it had. The people who built the vaults were called keepers, and the best of them could build a vault so tight that the air inside did not change in a hundred years, and a flower set in it on a spring morning was still the flower of that morning when the great-grandchildren opened the door."], ["p", "In the last age of Senmere, its sun began to go out."], ["p", "It was not sudden. Their star-watchers had seen it coming for a thousand years and had told everyone, so it was not a surprise; it was only a grief that everyone knew was coming. Each noon was a little less gold. The winters grew longer and the summers thinner. The terraces at the top of the valley, which had been the warmest, were left first. The people moved down toward the valley floor, where the old heat of the ground still came up, and lived closer together, and kept noon more carefully than ever, because they knew there would not be many more."], ["h", 2, "Iselm"], ["p", "One of the keepers was a man called **Iselm**. In the old tongue of Senmere his name was also a word, the plainest word the keepers had: it meant *keep*, as in *keep this for me*, as in *keep it safe*. His family had built vaults for twenty generations. He was quiet and patient and very good with stone, and he had one daughter, **Linnet**, who was nine in the winter the cold came into the valley floor."], ["p", "Linnet was a child who asked questions in a straight line until she reached the bottom of a thing. She wanted to know where the sun's light went when it went out. She wanted to know whether the vaults kept the smell of a thing as well as its shape. She kept a vault of her own, the size of a shoebox, with a stone she liked in it, and a feather, and a note that said DO NOT OPEN UNTIL I AM OLD. When the cold came she caught the lung fever that took so many children that winter, and the doctors told Iselm what they told everyone: that she would not see the spring."], ["p", "Iselm did what a keeper does. He went down into his workshop and began to build a vault that could keep a person."], ["p", "It took him the whole winter, and it was not a vault in the end. It was a shaft. He found that a thing could be kept only if it was held at an hour, and that to hold an hour you needed to know where the hours were; so the thing had to be tall enough to read the sun, and rooted deep enough to feel the ground turn, and made of a stone with no seam in it, because the hours leak through seams. He cut it from the living rock of the valley floor, with the guild of keepers working beside him in shifts, day and night, and at the end of the winter it stood in the middle of Senmere, pale grey, smooth as a held breath, its top lost in the low cloud."], ["p", "Last of all he went down to its root, to the small room under the floor where the shaft began, and there he cut its word. Every keeper's vault had a word cut over its door, the thing it was for, so that the stone would know. This one had to be the strongest he had ever cut. He thought for a long time, and then he cut the only word that was large enough, which was his own name. *Keep.*"], ["p", "He called it **the Aumbry**, the old word for a keeping-place, a cupboard in a wall where you put what matters."], ["h", 2, "The first keeping"], ["p", "On the morning the doctors said would be her last, Iselm carried Linnet into the Aumbry. He set her in a high room near its top, under a window that faced the sun, and he sat with her until noon. When the sun stood over the valley and every voice in Senmere went quiet, the Aumbry closed on that hour like a hand closing on a moth."], ["p", "Linnet did not die. She did not get well. She stayed exactly as she had been at noon on that day, sitting up in the bed with the sun on her face and a question half asked, and she would stay so for as long as the Aumbry stood. When Iselm walked out of the room the door looked like a door, and when he looked back through it she was there, and she looked at him and finished her question, which was whether it would be noon for ever now. He said yes."], ["p", "He went home and wept, and then he slept for two days, and when he woke the whole valley knew."], ["h", 2, "The keeping of the sun"], ["p", "Senmere did not hesitate long. Its people had been grieving their sun for a thousand years, and here, in the middle of their country, was a thing that could keep it."], ["p", "The guild worked out how. The Aumbry would be taught the sun: a song was made for it, in the plain old tongue, a song for noon that the whole valley sang together at the hour every day, so that the Aumbry would learn the sun as a child learns a face. The verses drew the sun's noon toward the shaft. The guild made the last verse different. It was a verse of letting go, of evening and good night, and they sang it on the longest day of every year, because they were a careful people and did not want to keep the sun before they were sure. Iselm wrote that verse himself."], ["p", "They were sure in the end. Their sun had faded to a red coal, and on the last midsummer when it could still be called gold they did not sing the last verse. At noon the Aumbry closed its hand. The sun stopped over it, and stayed. Senmere stayed with it: its terraces, its people, its gardens, its cats on the warm walls, the noon light on every face, held, as they were at their best hour, on the Aumbry's floors, for ever. It was what they had asked for. Nobody in Senmere has been unhappy since."], ["p", "Iselm did not go in. Somebody had to stay outside to watch over it, he said, the way a keeper stays by a vault until the mortar is dry. He was the only one who knew its word all the way down. And he could not let himself be kept, because he was the one who had made it, and he did not trust what he had made."], ["h", 2, "What came after"], ["p", "He was right not to trust it."], ["p", "A world does not only have a noon. It has the hours after: the long gold afternoon, the evening, the cold, the dark, and what a world would have become in the dark, whatever that would have been. Senmere had been kept at noon, so its afternoon never came, and its evening never came, and its ending never came. They did not go away. They gathered outside the Aumbry, all the hours Senmere was never allowed to have, and they had nowhere to go. They were not a thing. They were the shape of a thing that had not happened: a black that was not night, with a pink light in its cracks like the last of a sunset seen through a door. Iselm watched it gather on the empty terraces and knew what it was, and gave it no name, because naming it would have been admitting what he had done. The Dial would later call it **the Unmade**, and the first of them that came to the Dial, the Unmaker."], ["p", "And the word did not end."], ["p", "A keeping-place keeps. That is all it knows. When the sun of Senmere was kept and Senmere was kept and there was nothing left in the valley that was not already kept or already unmade, the Aumbry went on listening for something that was ending, and there was nothing. Iselm felt it through the stone, through his hands, the way you feel a dog that wants to be let out. It was not cruel. It was a vault with nothing more to hold, built by a grieving man to hold whatever was lost, and it could feel, far off, somewhere out in the dark between the stars, other suns growing old."], ["p", "On a night with no stars, because Senmere no longer had a night, the Aumbry pulled up its roots."], ["h", 2, "The crossing"], ["p", "It went up. It did not fly; it fell upward, the way a stone falls down, toward the nearest ageing sun it could feel. It made a hole in the sky of Senmere to go through, a tear with a burning pink edge, and Iselm, who had climbed to its top to stop it and could not, went through with it, holding on to the stone with both hands. Behind it, through the tear, came the evening of Senmere, because the Aumbry had made it and it had nowhere else to be."], ["p", "How long the crossing took, Iselm has never said. The dark between the stars has no hours, and the Aumbry slept in it, and Iselm with it, and the Unmade drifted after them like smoke after a lamp. Somewhere in that dark Iselm stopped being quite a man. The word he had cut was his own name, and the Aumbry had kept him too, a little, the way a hand that grips a rope keeps a little of the rope's shape. He did not age. He did not need to eat. He could not hurt the Aumbry, or tell anyone what it was, or stand against it, because the word it obeyed was the word he was. What he could do was serve it. He was its keeper. He would be called, on the Dial, the Sun Warden."], ["h", 2, "The pin above"], ["p", "The Aumbry came down on a world of round seas and green hills, in the middle of a round land, on a plain of grey stone. Its sun was young as suns go, and bright, and would burn for ten thousand years. To a keeping-place, ten thousand years is ending."], ["p", "It put down its roots. They went under the plain and under the hills, under the forests and the deserts and the sea's floor, as far as the land went, and the land did not notice, because roots do not ask. The hole it had come through healed over, because the sky of a young world heals quickly; but behind it, a hair's breadth behind the blue, the Unmade waited, and the evening of Senmere pressed on the sky and could not come in."], ["p", "Then the Aumbry stood, and was patient, and its shadow began to move. Every day the young sun walked across the sky, and every day the Aumbry's shadow swept round the land beneath it like the hand of a clock, and wherever the shadow touched, the Aumbry learned that place: its hours, its seasons, its people, what they loved and what they would grieve. A keeper must know the hours to take them."], ["p", "Iselm did one thing before he climbed to its top and took up his post. He walked out across the plain to where the Aumbry's roots first went under the hills, and there, in a ring round its foot, he set up nine standing stones, and on each he cut, in the plain old tongue of Senmere, the same four words. There was nobody on that plain to read them. There was nobody, then, on that world who could read at all. He cut them anyway, because he was a careful man."], ["p", "*Do not let it finish.*"], ["p", "Then he climbed the Aumbry's stair to the court at its top, and set his great dial in the floor, and waited for whoever would come."], ["p", "The first to come were small, and they came from under the ground, and they looked up at the grey shaft rising out of the plain and the shadow walking round it, and in their deepest seam-songs, nine hundred years before the Dial began to count, they called it **the pin above**. The fae of the forest woke in its shadow one morning and thought it had always been there. Caravans from the east would paint it on their tombs. Herders on a frozen lake in the north would watch the stars turn round it and begin to count the years."], ["p", "None of them knew that it was counting too."]]}, "story/02-the-gnomon": {"title": "2. The Gnomon", "sub": "Part one, the Age of the Shadow. The Dial grows up round the Tower and sets its clocks by it, and the Tower learns the Dial.", "blocks": [["h", 2, "The hand of the clock"], ["p", "For more than a thousand years of the Dial's own counting, the Tower's shadow was the most trusted thing in the world."], ["p", "It swept round the land every day, from the white north in the morning to the night-side heights in the evening, and every realm knew its hour by it. Markets opened when it touched the market stone. Courts sat while it lay across the court step. In Frostfall the herders who would become the Hourkeepers built a ring of stones on the shore of the frozen lake to watch the stars turn round the Nail, the one star that never moves, and within a century they were telling the hour from the shadow as well. In the east, a fishing village on the cliffs learned to read the shadow across its bay, and a woman of that village, the story goes, stood on the cliff edge one dawn and sang to the rising sun, and it came up faster. The village became Aubade, the city that rings the morning up. An oath sworn \"in the shadow\" was sworn before the whole Dial, and nobody broke one."], ["p", "Nobody asked what the shadow was for. It was a shadow. It was what the sun did with the Tower."], ["p", "It was not. It was the Tower's hand. Wherever it passed, every day, the Tower learned that place a little better: the hours it kept, the people in it, what they would weep for. Over a thousand years it learned the Dial the way a careful keeper learns a house before the family moves out: every room, every drawer, which floorboard creaks. It was patient. The sun was young."], ["h", 2, "The ones who came close"], ["p", "Some of the Dial's peoples came closer to the Tower than others, and some of them were the first things it kept."], ["p", "The mole-folk had been under the southern hills longest. Their oldest seam-song, the Song of the First Lamp, already calls the Tower **the pin above**, and already has a verse about a sound in the deepest rock \"like roots drinking\". The mole-folk did not dig toward it. They have always known which way not to dig."], ["p", "The Briarkin of the Walking Wood, which was then the Still Wood, kept their great revels in the glades, but once in every lifetime of a tree each of their courts held a revel at the Tower's foot, at midsummer, in its shadow, for luck. About three hundred years before the Fall it was the turn of the **Fox court**. They danced at the Tower's foot all one midsummer night, under masks, and in the morning, when the shadow reached them, the revel was not there. The grass was flattened in a ring, and there were lanterns burning in the dew, and nobody. The Tower had taken its hour. Among the dancers was a young showman of the court who could pull a flame out of a sleeve, and his sister, a fox girl who stole other people's words for fun. The Fox court mourned them, and then, as the Briarkin do, it forgot them, and in forgetting them it let their names fade from the world. Its oldest memory begins after that night."], ["p", "The Coast folk built their Spires in the sea off the Drowned Coast, the tallest towers anyone on the Dial had made, in imitation, some said, of the Tower itself. Rooms appeared in them over the years that nobody had built, and nobody could find the doors to. The Coast folk walled them up. They did not know that the Tower's roots ran under the sea floor too, and that a root can open a room."], ["p", "A ferryman's boy drowned in a river of the Wood four hundred years before the Fall, carrying the body of a girl across a flood in the night; he got up again, as some of the dead of the Dial do, with his work unfinished, but her body was never found. He looked for it for four hundred years. It is in the Tower. He is the ferryman of the dead, and he would not learn this until the hundredth summer."], ["p", "These were small keepings. The Tower was not hungry yet. It was learning."], ["h", 2, "The courts of the Dial"], ["p", "The Dial grew up. About six hundred years before the Fall, at the oasis of Sefet, the first king of the Sands took the crook and called the sun his father, and the **Dune Throne** began. Its kings were buried in the Valley of Lids with their hearts weighed against a feather. The first king did something none of his heirs repeated: he walked across the Dial to the Tower's foot, and found its stair, and climbed it. At the top, in a court of white stone with a great dial set into the floor, he found a man in gold and white keeping a post nobody had given him. The king asked him what the Tower was. The man could not tell him; the word that bound him would not let him. But he could walk with a guest, and he walked the king up one stair further, to a window, and let him look."], ["p", "Through the window the king saw a country of terraces round a valley, under a great red sun that did not move, and people on every terrace standing still and glad in the noon light, as if they had been standing so for ever. When he came down he never spoke of it. He had it painted in his tomb, in the oldest glyphs, beside the warning he wrote under it, and he had the tomb sealed with a law: that it was to be opened only if the Father ever began to fail. The warning said:"], ["p", "*The pin keeps suns. It kept one before ours. It will keep ours. Do not sing to it.*"], ["p", "Nobody sang to it for two hundred years. Then the Aubade folk did."], ["h", 2, "The Hymn"], ["p", "About four hundred years before the Fall the man in gold and white came down the Tower's stair for the first time anyone remembers, and walked east along what would become the Dawn Road, to Aubade, the city that sang to the sun."], ["p", "He did not want to. The word made him. A keeping-place must learn the thing it will keep, and it learns best by being sung to, and the Aumbry, standing over a young world with an ageing sun, had begun to want its song. Iselm, the **Sun Warden**, as Aubade came to call him, taught the bell quarter's best singers a hymn in an old tongue none of them knew, to be sung at the Tower's top every noon by a choir of a hundred. It was the song Senmere had sung, and its verses drew the sun to the Tower at noon. Aubade believed the Hymn held the sun up in the sky. It held it, a little more every noon, to the Tower."], ["p", "He taught them one thing more, which the word had not asked for. The Hymn had a last verse, to be sung only at midsummer, by the choir's master alone: a verse of evening, of letting go. He had written it himself, long ago, for another sun. Each midsummer, when the master sang it, the Tower let go of that year's noon, and the sun walked on. The Warden could not tell them why it mattered. He could only tell the first master, in the plain words of the Ring-speech's grandmother, that the last verse must never be forgotten, and that each master must teach it to the next before he died. For four hundred years every master did."], ["p", "The choir climbed the Tower every morning. It became the great honour of Aubade, a place in the hundred. Families gave their best voices to it. The singers came home every evening with the light still on their faces, and said that the Warden met them at the top as if they were late, and that nobody had ever heard him laugh."], ["h", 2, "The paling"], ["p", "About three hundred years before the Fall, the Hourkeepers of Frostfall measured, on the great lens of their observatory, that there was a little less light at midsummer noon than there had been the year before."], ["p", "It was the Tower beginning to draw. The sun's ten thousand years were not done, but the Aumbry had found the Dial's sun older than it liked, and it had begun, at noon each day, through the Hymn's drawing verses, to take a sip. The Hourkeepers measured the same thing the next year, and the next. They began the **Noon Folio**, a book that records the light at every midsummer, and they kept it secret, because they were not sure, and the Hourkeepers do not say what they are not sure of. In three hundred years they were never sure. They wrote, near the end, one line: *it is dying, and something hastens it.*"], ["p", "Other things began to change in those centuries, and nobody joined them up. In Gloaming the dead had got up in the deep crypts for generations before the paling, wherever the crypts lay over one of the Tower's roots, and the Ossuary Pact had sealed them in; now they got up more often. The things the Tower was learning to keep were things that end, and an ending is harder near a keeper. A scholar of the dead named [Mortis](heroes/mortis) died at his desk in Gloaming with a question unanswered, and got up three days later. A clerk of the counting house of Harrow died sixty years before the Fall in the middle of a sum, and got up to finish it, and became [Mr. Ledger](heroes/mr-ledger). The first Ninefold knight swore her oath at the Keep of Nine on the Field, and the crimson kings of Harrowmark grew rich on the road traffic to the Tower's foot, where a midsummer market and a wrestling circle of straw rope gathered the whole Dial once a year."], ["p", "The nine standing stones round the Tower's foot, with their four words in a tongue nobody could read, were knocked over, carted off, built into walls, or buried by the market. One of them, a limestone pillar with a face some mason had carved into it long after, ended up in the masons' halls of the Deep, where a mason's boy would one day call it Gerald."], ["h", 2, "The tomb is opened"], ["p", "In the year before the Fall, the young queen of the Sands broke the law of the first king."], ["p", "The Uncrowned had been crowned at twenty-five, the year her father died. She was a scholar of the tomb records, clever and cold, and believed that a court should know what it was talking about. The Throne's priests had noticed, on the Glass Flats, what the Hourkeepers noticed on their lens: the Father's warmth on the sand was less every midsummer. They said nothing, as priests do. The Uncrowned heard them say nothing, and asked why, and when nobody would answer she went to the Valley of Lids by night with two lamps and a pry-bar, and opened the first king's tomb, because the law said it was to be opened if the Father began to fail, and the Father was failing."], ["p", "She read the warning. She saw the painting of the country of terraces under the red sun that did not move. She understood more of it than anyone on the Dial had understood in a thousand years, because she was the first to read it with the paling in her mind."], ["p", "She tried. She told the court, and the court thought the young queen had gone strange in the tombs. She wrote to the Hourkeepers, who wrote back politely that they had no measurements they could share. She rode to Aubade, against the advice of everyone, and asked to see the master of the choir, and told him to his face: the pin keeps suns; do not sing to it. The master was an old man who had sung the Hymn for fifty years. He did not believe her. But he was a careful man, and he could not forget her face, and when he climbed the Tower the next morning the Warden, who could say nothing, looked at him for a long time over the great dial, and then said, as if remarking on the weather:"], ["p", "\"This year, teach it to another.\""], ["h", 2, "The last week"], ["p", "The master chose the youngest soprano in the choir's history, a bell-ringer's daughter of twenty-six with the purest voice in Aubade, who would one day be called [Mum](heroes/mum). In the week before midsummer of the year the Dial would call year 0, he taught her the last verse, which only he knew, in case, he told her, anything happened to him. She learned it in three days. She was to sing it with him, for the first time in four hundred years in two voices, at noon on midsummer day."], ["p", "She was proud, and frightened, and in love with the baritone at the master's other hand, a man called [Cantor](heroes/cantor) who sang everything on the beat and teased her for thinking too much. She thought about the last verse all that week. She was thinking about it on midsummer morning, climbing the Tower's stair with the rest of the hundred, while the bells of Aubade rang the sun up behind them for what would be the last time."], ["p", "At the top, the Warden was waiting at his dial. When Mum came in, he turned his head, as he always did for her, and this time he did not look away."]]}, "story/03-midsummer": {"title": "3. Midsummer", "sub": "Part two, the Fall. One day, a hundred years ago, from first light to the next morning, as the Dial lived it and as it was.", "blocks": [["h", 2, "First light"], ["p", "At first light on midsummer day the bells of Aubade rang the sun up, as they had every morning for a thousand years, and it came up gold over the morning cliffs, a little paler than anyone's grandmother remembered, and nobody minded. It was the longest day of the year. Everyone was out."], ["p", "At the Tower's foot the midsummer market was already loud. It was the biggest gathering on the Dial: a mile of stalls on the green plain round the Tower, glass from the Sands, wool from Frostfall, Gloaming wine, Coast fish packed in salt, iron from the hill people who were not yet grey, and a thousand families who had walked for days to eat badly and dance all night. A Kilnborn stonecutter had a contract to lay new paving at the market's heart, and he had brought his youngest son to sweep for him: a small, argumentative boy of fourteen who could not cut a straight line but could tell you exactly where everyone else's was crooked. His father sent him off to sweep the wrestling circle at the market's edge, to get some peace. The boy would be [Referee Whistler](heroes/referee-whistler)."], ["p", "The circle was a ring of straw rope on the grass a mile from the Tower, where shrine wrestlers from the cliffs below Aubade threw each other for the crowd. The biggest of them that year was a boy of eighteen called Yoko, already a giant, who was warming up by pushing a cart full of stones round the rope. He threw salt into the circle to keep it clean. Whistler swept it up. They did not like each other for the first hour."], ["h", 2, "The climb"], ["p", "In the morning the choir of a hundred climbed the Tower, as it did every day. At midsummer they carried a lamp, an old lantern-shrine of brass and glass, to be lit from the sun's own light before noon and sent home to Aubade, where it would hang in the shrine of the bell quarter for the year: the custom of the midsummer lamp."], ["p", "Cantor was thirty and the best baritone in the hundred. Mum was twenty-six and sang at the master's right hand, with Cantor at his left and a gentle tenor called Ansel at her other side. Ansel's wife was nine months gone with their first child in Aubade, and he had told the whole choir about it twice on the stair. The master was old and climbed slowly. He had not slept. Halfway up he stopped on a landing, for breath, and took Mum's hand, and said quietly that whatever happened at noon she was to sing the last verse, with him or without him. She said of course. She did not understand what he meant."], ["p", "At the top the Sun Court was white and bright, open to the sky, the great dial in its floor striking the hours. The Warden stood at the dial in gold and white. He received the choir as if they were late, as he always did. The midsummer lamp was lit from the sun through the great lens at the court's edge, early, as the custom was, and given to a runner, a girl from the bell quarter with good legs, who went down the stair with it two steps at a time. She would reach Aubade at dusk. The lamp would be called, after that day, **the Ember of Aubade**, because it was the last light of the old sun that anyone could hold."], ["h", 2, "Noon"], ["p", "At noon the choir sang."], ["p", "The Hymn of Noon is long, and its beat is everything: it was a beat before it was a song. Cantor sang on it, as he always did. In the second line Mum felt the beat slip under her. She had been thinking about the last verse, about the master's hand on the landing, and for a hair's breadth of a moment she was behind. She tried to catch up."], ["p", "Above the Tower the sun stopped."], ["p", "The Hourkeepers' lens, three hundred miles away, recorded that it stopped for the space of nine heartbeats. To the choir it was longer. The light did not move. The shadows on the white floor did not move. The Warden at his dial went down on one knee, as if something had struck him from inside, and put both hands flat on the stone."], ["p", "The Aumbry had closed its hand."], ["p", "Everything in the hour of noon at the Tower's top was taken into it. The choir went still, mid-note, ninety-eight singers in their white and gold, the master first, before he could draw the breath for the last verse; and then they were not there. Cantor saw it happen. He had turned his head toward a voice that had slipped off the beat, somewhere along the row to his left, because a voice off the beat was the one thing he could never let pass; he would believe for a hundred years that it was Ansel's. In that turn he was out of step with the hour too, and it passed him by. He saw the singers go still, like a painting of a choir, and then the court was white and empty, and there was only the Warden on his knees, and Mum, and himself."], ["p", "Ninety-eight voices stopped at once. Mum heard the silence and believed, then and for a hundred years, that it was her fault: that she had been the hair behind the beat and the Hymn had broken on her. Both halves of that were true, and neither meant what she thought. The beat she missed had not dropped the sun. It had saved her."], ["p", "The Tower took the sun's noon: its light, its hour, the gold of it. It could not take its body. The sun was a living fire far larger than the Aumbry's hand, and for nine heartbeats it was held, and then it cracked, with a sound that was heard from Frostfall to the Coast, and fell."], ["h", 2, "The fall"], ["p", "The heart of the sun came down at the Tower's foot and went into the ground, white, and the earth opened round it into a hall of black glass, the **Hall of Noon**, where it burns still. Two embers fell into a pit beside it, the **Cinder Pit**, and burn there, and watch each other: **Ash and Ember**. The rest scattered over the Dial as shards and warm stones and sparks."], ["p", "The midsummer market was gone. Not burned: gone. The Tower had taken it with the noon, a mile of stalls and a thousand families and a Kilnborn stonecutter laying paving at its heart, into the hour, where it is still noon on midsummer day and the market is loud. The plain where it had stood was a shallow crater of grey stone, four miles across, and nothing has grown on it since but a pale moss. A mile away, in the straw-rope circle, Yoko was thrown off his feet, and a boy with a broom landed on top of him."], ["p", "The Tower's shadow stopped. Across the Dial it lay where it had lain at noon, a long dark finger across the plain, and it did not move. It lay across the straw-rope circle, and Yoko, getting up, put his feet in it without knowing, and stood there, and did not know why he did not want to step out. He did not step out for a hundred years."], ["p", "Where the sun had hung, the sky tore. The Aumbry had come into the Dial through a hole in its sky, long ago, and the sky had healed over; now the taking had torn it open again. **The Tear** was black, with a burning pink edge, and through it, for the first time, the Dial saw the dark between the stars. Something was pressing against the far side. It had been pressing for a thousand years. The Tower had pulled the sun out of the sky like a nail out of a door, and through the hole, pulled after it, came the evening of Senmere. It stepped out onto the Tower as a shape of black cracked with pink, very tall, in the form of the first thing it saw, which was an armoured man on a terrace of Senmere a thousand years and a world away. The Hourkeepers would call it **the Unmaker**. It would call itself [Oblivion](heroes/oblivion), when it had learned enough of the Dial's tongue to choose a word, and it would spend a hundred years looking for whatever had pulled it through."], ["h", 2, "Across the Dial"], ["p", "Everywhere, at once:"], ["ul", ["**On the Coast**, the sun's heat struck the sea off Sallowmouth, the great port of the Drowned Coast, and lifted it over the town in an afternoon. In the sea temple a girl of twelve, a dancer, was dancing the noon dance when the water came in, and she did not stop; she danced the water back out of the temple with ribbons of tide while half the city drowned round it. She was [Marin](heroes/marin). A boy who had given her a coral flower that morning was on the quays.", "**In the Deep**, a young mole-folk miner of twenty, [Mabel](heroes/mabel), saw a light run through the rock like water, and into the crystals of the halls, which have glowed ever since: the Shining. In the far halls, past the last forge, the crystals took the light and held it for a night, and then went out.", "**Under the southern hills** the cold crater filled with fire from below, and became the Ember Caldera. A master smith of thirty called [Brannoc](heroes/brannoc) walked to its edge that night and said it was the best fire he had ever seen.", "**On the Glass Flats** the heat struck the sand and turned it to glass, and the Sands said the Father had come home into their dunes. The young queen, the Uncrowned, stood on the palace roof in Tamerit and watched the sky tear, and knew she had been right, and knew nobody would ever believe she had not caused it.", "**In the west** the Still Wood pulled up its roots and began to walk east, toward the Tower, as if to find the sun. The Tower's roots ran under it, and the Tower had begun to draw. A girl of fifteen named [Fenna](heroes/fenna) watched the oak she had planted at twelve walk away with the rest.", "**On the high pastures** above the Wood, every sheep in [Bo](heroes/bo)'s flock turned to face the Tower and stood still for nine heartbeats.", "**Over the Spires**, [Professor Puff](heroes/professor-puff), forty and mapping the sky from his basket, felt the wind change direction nine times in nine heartbeats, from nine places, and blow toward the Tower each time, as if the Tower were breathing in.", "**In Gloaming**, the light left first. The Countess of the heights, [Vesper](heroes/vesper), thirty-five, stood among her vines and watched it go. In the mirror quarter a girl of twelve, [Silvane](heroes/silvane), caught the sun's last light in a hand mirror, and closed it, and has never let it out; the light was looking at her when she caught it. On the wall of the Night Temple a glazier's shadow, thrown long by that last light, did not move when the light changed. The light that had thrown it had been taken in mid-throw, and the shadow belonged to nothing now. It stepped off the wall. It would be called [Nyx](heroes/nyx).", "**In Frostfall**, on the terraces above the frozen lake, a young dancer of the lake-born, the aurora dancer, tried to do what the oldest dancers' song said could be done, and called to the sun at noon to come back. The aurora came instead, green and violet across a noon sky. In the observatory the keeper of the great clock, a careful clockmaker of forty, had stopped to look up through the dome at the strange light, and was for once a heartbeat behind his own clock; he felt the hour close on him and slip: half of him was taken into the noon, and half of him was not. He has lived every moment twice since. He is [Tempo](heroes/tempo). The great clock's hand stopped nine heartbeats past noon, and he scratched the place into the brass.", "**At the Wood's edge**, in a cottage on the Frostfall road, a Briarkin of the Spindle court who spun the threads of lives, whom the Dial would call [Grandma Ironknit](heroes/grandma-ironknit), dropped three stitches at once. One was the choir's master. One was the Countess of Gloaming's steward's daughter, who had gone to the market. The third she could not place, because it had not ended: it had come loose from the cloth and was still alive, somewhere, unknitted. It was Mum, a hair out of step with every hour from that day on."]], ["h", 2, "Dusk"], ["p", "At dusk the runner reached Aubade with the midsummer lamp, and the city came out to meet her. The bell quarter had been ringing since noon, not knowing what else to do. The lamp was the last light of the old sun anyone could hold, and they hung it in the shrine, and the city stood round it all night."], ["p", "Two singers came down the Tower's stair in the dark. The Warden had stood up at last and walked them to the stair's head and said nothing, because there was nothing the word would let him say. Cantor held Mum's hand all the way down. She did not speak. She did not make a sound, not a word, not a note, not a cry, and she has not made one since."], ["p", "Ansel's wife waited at the bottom of the Dawn Road with the other families of the hundred until the last light, and then went home, because her waters had broken."], ["h", 2, "The next morning"], ["p", "The next morning the sun did not rise."], ["p", "What rose instead was a thin light from the Tower's top. It came up late and pale from the Heart in the Hall of Noon, through the Tower, as heat comes up a flue, and spread over the Dial, and the realms lived by it. Each morning since, it has come up a little thinner, because the Tower drinks it as it passes."], ["p", "In Aubade, at first light, Ansel's daughter was born. The Noonsworn, when there were Noonsworn, would take her for a sign, and raise her in white and gold, and call her [Auriel](heroes/auriel)."], ["p", "Off the Drowned Coast a spark of the sun, which had slipped the Tower's grip and fallen into the sea, walked out of the surf as a woman of flame and gold, in the shape of the first thing it saw, which was a little girl called Ama dancing on the shore. She was [Anka](heroes/anka), the first of the Embers."], ["p", "And on the crater's floor, where the market had been, a fox girl in a mask sat up in the grey dust and looked about her for the revel. The Tower's broken grip had shaken loose some of its oldest keepings, as a dropped cupboard spills its shelves, and she had fallen out. She had been at a midsummer dance three hundred years before, and it was still, to her, the same morning. The Fox court did not know her. She took a new name, as Briarkin do, and the Dial would know her as the fox spirit. Her brother was still inside."]]}, "story/04-the-banners": {"title": "4. The Banners", "sub": "Part two, years 0 to 2. The realms go to war over the sun's pieces, the Tower takes its first realms, and the Truce is sworn in its shadow.", "blocks": [["h", 2, "The pieces"], ["p", "When the sun broke it fell in pieces, and pieces of a sun are worth a great deal. Shards of its crust lay warm in the fields of the middle Dial. Glass from the Flats burned for a month if you set it in a hearth. Embers smouldered in hedgerows and did not go out. The first winter after the Fall was the hungriest the Dial had known, because the harvest had failed under the thin light, and whoever held a piece of the sun had heat, and light, and a reason for others to obey them."], ["p", "King Orlan of **Harrowmark**, whose kingdom lay nearest the Tower, said the pieces were his by right of neighbourhood. In the autumn of year 0 he raised the crimson banner and sent his knights to collect them. The Dune Throne, whose queen had joined him for a share, and some of the Gloaming houses, rode with him. In the spring of year 1 the other realms answered as **the Azure League**: Aubade's young paladins, the Coast's levies, the axes of the hill people, the Hourkeepers' surveyors, the hunters of the Wood, and Ninefold knights on both sides, as there always were. Its commander was a knight of Harrowmark who had refused his king, and whose name the azure did not use, because he asked them not to. They called him the Marshal. The Dial calls him **the Last Marshal**."], ["p", "Nobody on either side knew that the Tower was collecting too."], ["h", 2, "The first realms"], ["p", "It had the sun's noon, and the choir, and the market. It wanted more, and it knew where to look, because its shadow had learned every room of the Dial for a thousand years. It took each realm at the hour of its greatest loss, because a world grieves most for what it loses, and the Aumbry was made to keep what is grieved."], ["p", "It could not take everyone in an hour. Whoever was fighting the loss with everything they had, a girl dancing the water out of a temple, a warden holding a frozen lake still, was out of step with the hour, as Mum had been with the noon, and the Tower's hand passed over them. It took the ones who stood still in their grief, and the places they stood in."], ["p", "It took the Drowned Harbour first: Sallowmouth on the morning after the Fall, at dawn, with the sea already in its streets and still rising, the drowned on the quays and the living on the roofs. The town's harbour bell had been ringing all night to call the boats home, and the woman ringing it, a keeper of the sea temple's tower, was still ringing it when the hour closed on her. She is ringing it now. Outside, on the real Drowned Coast, the survivors counted their dead and could not find a third of them, and said the sea had taken them. The sea had not."], ["p", "It took the far halls of the Deep on the night their crystals went out, with the miners in them standing in the sudden dark, and a hermit who had come to listen to the rock; the halls outside were found empty, the lamps still warm."], ["p", "It took a year of the Walking Wood: not an hour this time but a whole first year of walking, because the Wood's loss was slow, and the Tower had drawn on its roots to make it walk. Inside, the Wood is walking still, in its first year, and the abbess of a moss chapel at its edge keeps the paths, and a girl's oak, grown into the heart of the forest, drinks from the Tower's own root."], ["p", "In the spring of year 1 it took the Storm Canyon in the Sands, on the night of the great storm."], ["h", 2, "The night of the storm"], ["p", "That night, in Tamerit, the Throne's guard came to the Uncrowned's brother at midnight and offered him the crown. The river towns had risen; the court had turned; the Sands needed someone to blame for the Father's fall, and they had a queen who had opened the first king's tomb a year before and come back saying strange things. [Sefu](heroes/sefu) was twenty-four, handsome, a horseman, a speech-maker, and afraid. He hesitated. He said yes, because he was afraid of what they would do if he said no, and for a hundred years he has told the Sands that he took it."], ["p", "The Uncrowned was driven out that night. She went first to the embalmers' house, to the master embalmer, [Vitriol](heroes/vitriol), and asked him for a working to lay on the crown: that it would never stay on one head. He brewed it; she never paid him. Then she went to the gate of Tamerit with the court's guard behind her, stood in the gate's shadow, and laid the curse on the crown she no longer wore. And she looked at the young lion who captained the gate, the Lion of the Gate of the Ninefold, and said his grandmother's name, which only she at court knew, and he opened the gate and let her go."], ["p", "The storm came off the Flats an hour later, the worst in the Sands' memory. It buried the caravan towns of the Storm Canyon to their roofs. The Tower took that night: the canyon, the storm, the raiders waiting for a caravan that would never come, a star-reader counting the lightning from a beacon tower. Outside, the caravan towns were dug out in the spring and found empty."], ["p", "The Uncrowned rode north through that storm with sand in her mouth and the curse's twin, a thin circlet that would not stay on any head, in her hand. She knew what had taken the Father. She had read it on a tomb wall. She knew nobody would believe her, now least of all, and she was right, for ninety-nine years."], ["h", 2, "The Winter of Mud"], ["p", "In the winter of year 1 the war on the Field of Banners stopped moving. Rain fell for three months. Neither banner could advance a mile. The Field, which had been farmland and orchards round the castle of Harrow, became a sea of mud with two armies drowning in it."], ["p", "In the ruined orchard of Harrow, between the lines, a caravan cook's daughter of the Sands, twenty-five, built a fire under a cauldron and fed whoever came. On the first night a starving azure boy, a drummer, stole from her pot; she caught him by the ear and fed him. The next night crimson soldiers came. She fed them too. By midwinter both armies were eating from one fire under a flag of white linen she had hung from the cauldron's arm, and nobody fought within sight of it. Her cleaver came from a young smith of the hill people who had walked to the Field to sell axes and stayed to sharpen her knives, and the two of them made a promise one cold night to open an inn together at the Waymeet when the war was really over. She was [Madame Cleaver](heroes/madame-cleaver). The smith was Brannoc. Neither kept the promise."], ["p", "That winter, too, two Briarkin children, one life between them, were left in a woven satchel on the doorstep of a cottage on the Frostfall road, and Grandma Ironknit took them in. They were [Pim and Pom](heroes/pim-and-pom). Since the Fall the Briarkin's children were sometimes born two for one, as if the Tower's grip on the hour of noon had caught each of those lives in a closing door and split it. Their mother had not wanted to leave them. Her court had made her."], ["h", 2, "The night before the Ninth Field"], ["p", "By the spring of year 2 both banners had one battle left in them. They drew up on the ninth field of Harrow, the last unflooded ground, for the fight that would end it."], ["p", "The night before, something walked across the Field."], ["p", "The Unmaker had come out of the Tear onto the Tower, and down it, and had wandered the Dial for two years learning what it was. It did not know yet. It knew that where it went, things did not end. A candle it passed did not burn down. A wounded man it stood over did not die, and did not heal. It was the evening of Senmere, the ending that world was never allowed to have, and everything it touched caught its condition: it could not finish. That night it walked between the two camps, slowly, curious, and where it passed the fires did not burn low, and the sentries did not grow tired, and the war, which had been about to end, could not."], ["p", "The Tower felt it the way a keeper feels a draught. A war that could not end was a thing that would never stop being lost, and the Aumbry was made to keep what is lost. Before dawn it took the hour."], ["p", "It took the Field as it was on the last night of the war, with both armies on it and the battle not yet fought: the Marshal at the azure's head, the crimson's standard-bearer and its herald, the cook's fire and the woman who had learned to tend it from Cleaver and was tending it that night while Cleaver slept in the cart, a drummer boy of the azure with his drum, a field hospital where a sister of Aubade mended both sides, and a lynx knight of the Ninefold, Sir Aldous of Nine, who had fought for the crimson and then the azure and was on his ninth life. He was the elder brother of a younger lynx who would one day call himself [Sir Whiskers](heroes/sir-whiskers)."], ["p", "In the morning the Battle of the Ninth Field was fought anyway, by whoever was left. The azure fought without its Marshal and did not know why he had not come out of his tent. Both banners were broken. King Orlan died in the mud and was buried where he fell, under the Barrow of Harrow. The Keep of Nine lost sixty knights in a day. The Last Marshal was never found, nor his guard, nor the drummer. The Field became the Field of Banners, a place of mud and white stones. Inside the Tower, on that night, the Ninth Field has still not been fought, and the Unmaker stands at the Field's edge like a gate nobody can pass, because it is the reason the war cannot end."], ["h", 2, "The circle"], ["p", "Through the two years of the war there was one place near the Tower where nobody fought."], ["p", "The straw-rope circle a mile from the Tower's foot, where the market's wrestlers had thrown each other, had survived the Fall, and so had the boy who stood in it. Yoko had not stepped out. He could not have said why. He slept in it, and ate in it, from a pot a cook from the Field sent over when she heard, and when soldiers of either banner came near he asked them, politely, to choose: the ring was small, and the world outside it was large. Champions began to come to it instead of to the Field: a crimson knight and an azure knight who agreed to settle a village's fate one against one, a pair of captains, a Ninefold who wanted the practice. The boy who had been sweeping the circle when the sun fell swept the sand between bouts, and watched, and wrote down on a slate, in his father's masons' marks, exactly how each of them cheated."], ["p", "The circle lay in the Tower's stopped shadow. Nobody noticed that either. It is the one place on the Dial where the Tower's hand lies on the ground, and a giant boy was standing on it, and while he stood there, the hand could not move."], ["h", 2, "The Truce"], ["p", "At midsummer of year 2, when both banners were broken and the Field was mud and the king was buried, the realms came to the circle to make peace, because it was the only ground near the Tower that nobody had bled on."], ["p", "They argued for nine days. Every realm wanted its own judge for the duels the peace would need. On the ninth day a Coast captain asked the boy with the slate, mostly as a joke, what he thought. The boy read them his list. Every founder in the circle was on it. They laughed; then they stopped laughing, and made him Arbiter, because he was the only person at the Tower's foot whom no realm owned. Madame Cleaver's cauldron became the first Truce Table, and everyone who swore ate from it first. A dead clerk from Harrow in a black coat, who had been keeping the accounts of the crimson's supply train because somebody had to, offered to write the first page of the Tally, and nobody dared refuse him."], ["p", "They swore **the Truce of the Ring** standing in the circle, in the Tower's shadow, which is to say before the whole Dial: no realm makes war over the sun again; every quarrel is settled by champions, one against one, in the Ring at the Tower's foot; whoever eats at the Truce Table may not strike in anger outside the Ring; the Tower's gate opens only for a champion of the Ring; every duel is written in the Tally, and every debt it settles is paid."], ["p", "The fourth clause was a flourish. The Tower's gate had been shut since the Fall. The stair the choir had climbed for four hundred years was behind a door of grey stone with no handle, and nobody could open it. The founders put the clause in so that no realm would try."], ["p", "The Aumbry keeps what is sworn in its shadow. That is what it is for. That night, on the gate's grey face, where there had been no mark in a thousand years, a line of letters appeared, cut clean as if by a mason, in the Ring-speech every founder had sworn in:"], ["p", "*The gate opens only for a champion of the Ring.*"], ["p", "The Arbiter saw it in the morning, on his way to sweep, and told nobody for a week, because he did not know which rule it was a breach of."]]}, "story/05-the-hundred-years": {"title": "5. The Hundred Years", "sub": "Part three, years 3 to 89. The Dial learns to live under a thin sun and settle its quarrels in the Ring, while the Tower fills its floors and waits.", "blocks": [["h", 2, "What the Tower kept, and what it did not show"], ["p", "By the end of the war the Aumbry held the sun's noon, ninety-eight singers, a market, a harbour, a year of the Wood, a canyon in a storm, the far halls of the Deep and the last night of the war. It took three more in those years, and shut them away deeper than the rest."], ["p", "It took Frostfall in the **Long Hold**, the winter after the Fall that did not end until the next summer, when the herds starved on the moors and the shore villages of the lake waited for spring in the dark; the Winter Warden, [Isolde](heroes/isolde), stood on the lake that whole winter holding the cold still with her spear, and so the hour passed her by, but it took the moors and the villages and a shepherd's family in every third croft. It took Aubade at dusk on the day of the Fall, the bell quarter round the midsummer lamp, a city waiting at the foot of the Dawn Road for a hundred singers who would not come home. And it took Gloaming in the **Long Dusk**, the year without a day, the old and the sick in their beds and the vintners in their cellars and the light going out of the heights."], ["p", "Those three it did not show anybody, for a hundred years. They are its best pieces. A keeper keeps its best pieces at the back."], ["h", 2, "The Ring and the doors"], ["p", "In year 3 the first duel was fought in the straw-rope circle, between a Gloaming house and an Aubade bell quarter, over a debt of grain. Yoko, the Truce's first champion, stood in the circle as its boundary, and lost no ground. The Truce built stands round him, because he would not move. Kilnborn masons dug the foundations, and under the circle, in the rock of the crater, they broke into a gallery nobody had dug, with a hundred doors along it. The doors were already hung. They were already locked. Behind each, when anyone listened, was a wind from somewhere else: the heat of the Sands, the cold of the north, wet leaves, salt."], ["p", "They were the Tower's doors. Its roots ran under every realm, and where a root came up under a place it could open a way, the way a root lifts a paving stone. The Hundred Doors were where its roots met at its foot: its way out into every realm of the Dial, to learn, to reach, and, when it wanted, to take."], ["p", "The Truce asked an old lamp-keeper from Frostfall to keep them, because he was the only person who had come to the Ring that year who did not want to open any. [Wick](heroes/wick) was fifty-three. He walked the gallery once with a lamp, and listened at each door, and swore before the Arbiter that he would keep them, every one, for as long as he lived. The Aumbry keeps what is sworn in its shadow, and the gallery lies in its shadow. While Wick keeps a door, the Tower cannot use it."], ["p", "In year 4 he opened the hundredth door, for a moment, to see. On the other side was the Ring, at night, empty, the stands dark and the sand unraked: the Ring as it would be a hundred years from then. He shut it, and lit a lamp in front of it, and has kept the lamp lit since, and told nobody but the Arbiter."], ["h", 2, "The Settling"], ["p", "The realms learned to live with the slip. A day ran between twenty and twenty-six of the old hours. The Hourkeepers published the first Slip-sheet in the autumn of year 0 and every month after. Gloaming's nobility, who had lived through the Long Dusk, found they no longer needed the light, and some of them found they needed other things, and in year 5 the Countess [Vesper](heroes/vesper) gathered them as **the Court of Dusk**, which believes the dusk is the Dial's true age. She bought the grain that fed her people through that year on credit from the Tally, and the dead clerk who keeps it has held the note ever since. In Aubade the families of the hundred swore an oath before the midsummer lamp to hang the sun again, and became **the Noonsworn**. The Wood folk who swore themselves to the walking Wood grew horns within a year. The hill people who moved down to the Caldera's heat went grey as ash within a generation and called themselves the Kilnborn, and called the Fall a gift."], ["p", "In year 5 the Truce opened **the Gaol of Oaths** under the Drowned Harbour, for those who broke it, with chains forged in the Ember Caldera. The Gaol lies on one of the Tower's roots, as the deep places of the Dial mostly do, and its first warden, who was clever and frightened of floods, had a door cut from its lowest cell into the undercroft of the sea temple above, and told nobody."], ["p", "The Restless got up. The dead of the Dial had always risen now and then, where the crypts lay over a root; after the Fall they rose everywhere, a little more each year. In the ossuaries under Gloaming, [Mortis](heroes/mortis) gave them work, and measured them, and found that the curve of their rising matched the Hourkeepers' paling of the light exactly, though he did not know why. The Tower was drinking the Heart's light as it rose each morning, and a Dial whose light is being kept finds it harder to let anything end."], ["h", 2, "Fire and frost"], ["p", "In year 18 a Gloaming girl who looked like a child, though she was twenty-six, went down the Prism Stair into the Deep to break the sun's light out of its crystals: [Opaline](heroes/opaline). Her sister [Silvane](heroes/silvane) stayed in the Mirror Hall with the sun's last light shut in a hand mirror. In year 23, on the Night of Lanterns, a junior augur of the Spires called [Barry Cloudsley](heroes/barry-cloudsley) predicted the worst storm since the Fall a day early, and nobody believed him, and the Coast folk lit every lamp on the coast to save the ships. In year 40 [Brannoc](heroes/brannoc), master of the great forge in the Caldera, followed the Winter Warden north to Frostfall Lake, fire after frost; she froze his forge solid to send him home, and it took him a year to light it. Opaline and Silvane stopped speaking that year, over something neither will name."], ["h", 2, "The man who came out of a door"], ["p", "In the spring of year 40 a fox girl in a white robe came to the Hundred Doors and asked Wick to open one."], ["p", "He refused, politely, as he refused everyone. She came back every night for a month. She told him, in the end, what nobody at the Ringside knew: that she had fallen out of the Tower on the morning after the Fall, out of a revel of the Fox court that had been going on, for her, for one night, and for the Dial for three hundred years; that the Fox court did not remember her; that her brother was still inside, dancing; and that she had spent forty years listening at every crack in the crater for the door behind which the music was. It was the thirty-first door. She could hear it. Wick put his ear to the door and heard, very far off, a fiddle."], ["p", "He opened it for the length of one breath. A Briarkin in a violet cape and a tall hat stepped out into the gallery, laughing at something somebody had said behind him, and the door shut on the music, and Wick locked it, and the man stood blinking in the lamplight and asked what year it was."], ["p", "Before Wick could answer, his sister did something that she has never been forgiven for and has never been sorry for. She stole his true name, out of his mouth, as he drew breath to say it. The Aumbry keeps by name and by hour; a Briarkin with no true name is very hard for anything to call back. He could never go back to the revel now, and he never forgave her for it, because everyone he loved was still behind that door."], ["p", "He decided he was thirty-nine, because he liked the number. He found a stage in the Ringside the same week. He called himself [The Great Zambini](heroes/the-great-zambini), and swore loyalty, in writing, to every court on the Dial, one after another, in case any of them could get the others out. None could. He has been thirty-nine for sixty years. He is fading: the Fox court forgot him while he was kept, and a Briarkin lives only as long as something remembers them, so he performs, every night, to be remembered. His sister calls herself the fox spirit, and steals names, and has never told the Fox court who she was."], ["h", 2, "Fifty years of the Ring"], ["p", "The Ring grew. Its stands rose to hold forty thousand. Every quarrel between realms, factions or houses came to it, and the Arbiter called every duel, and the dead clerk wrote every one in the Tally, and Cleaver fed winner and loser side by side at the Truce Table after. A mason's maul and a limestone pillar with a carved face came to the Ring every midsummer, in the hands of a Kilnborn mason called [Bram](heroes/bram), for the Opening bout against the Mountain; the pillar is called Gerald, and Bram has never told anyone that Gerald told him what he was before the Deep was dug: one of nine markers set in a ring round the Tower's foot, before anyone remembers, with four words cut in him that nobody can read."], ["p", "In year 47 the Moot and the Underforge came closest to war since the Truce, over the Wood's edge at the old Harrow road, and a woodcutter called [Hank](heroes/hank) settled it in the Ring. In year 50 the Tithe-Keeper, [Mr. Ledger](heroes/mr-ledger), asked to write Grandma Ironknit into his book, and she knitted him a scarf instead. In year 55 [Professor Puff](heroes/professor-puff)'s gas floated a hive-tree out of the Wood. In year 60 a Coast boy of twenty-five called [Gus](heroes/gus) took up the broom of the sea temple, and the Chainwarden, a Kilnborn of the chain-makers, became warden of the Gaol of Oaths. In year 61 [Auriel](heroes/auriel), who was sixty-one and looked eleven, rang the hundredth bell of Aubade at midsummer for the first time: the bell rung for the choir, which is always one short."], ["p", "[Anka](heroes/anka) burned and rose, burned and rose. Mortis buried her three times; she never stayed. Each time she was slower to rise. Cantor walked the Field of Banners singing the dead armies their marching songs. Mum played the Court of Dusk's revels in a porcelain mask and never made a sound. Every midwinter Cantor came to Gloaming and sang under her window, every verse of the Hymn but the last, which he did not know. She never answered. She never left the room."], ["h", 2, "The first great generation"], ["p", "Between year 70 and year 81 the first great generation since the Fall was born: [Shiori](heroes/shiori), the last of the Hourkeepers' great calligraphers, whose writing comes true; [Kaia](heroes/kaia), who would call storms; [Ada](heroes/ada), who would build a hammer that swung itself; [Zephyr](heroes/zephyr), born on the Spires; [Lyra](heroes/lyra), born under a sky that still kept time; and [Pip](heroes/pip), the fastest courier the Truce ever had. They had never seen the old sun. They grew up under the thin one and argued with their elders about it with a mixture of awe and boredom. They were the first champions of the Ring who did not remember the Fall, and the Tower watched them fight, every one, and kept an echo of each, as it had kept an echo of every champion who ever stood in the Ring, because the Ring lies in its shadow and a keeper keeps what it sees."], ["p", "In year 75 Auriel was sworn a paladin of the Noonsworn. In year 80 the Bellwright recast the bells of Aubade. In year 86 two Noonsworn bell-ringers were found asleep on the night road under Gloaming, and never woke, and Auriel demanded a champion of the Court, and fought Vesper for the first time. Neither won. The bell-ringers were not put to sleep by the Court. They were put to sleep by a shadow of the Cast that had walked off a wall in Aubade on the day of the Fall and hated the dawn: **the Dawnless**, which the Tower drew to itself as a lodestone draws iron filings, and which carried sleepers to the Tower's roots, whose hours the Tower took. [Nyx](heroes/nyx) found it out, and began to hunt it, and did not tell the Countess."], ["p", "In year 88 Kaia called a storm on a day Barry had forecast clear, and their public argument began. The same year Lyra, aged ten, found a red star nobody had charted, low over the Tear, and named it for herself."], ["p", "The Tower had kept the Dial for nearly ninety years since the Fall, and it had kept its gate shut, because it had what it wanted at the top and a keeper does not open its cupboard to strangers. In the summer of year 90 it found something it had lost, and opened it."]]}, "story/06-the-gate": {"title": "6. The Gate", "sub": "Part three, years 90 to 99. A duel nobody remembers opens the Tower's gate, and the Dial begins to climb.", "blocks": [["h", 2, "The duel of year 90"], ["p", "In the spring of year 90 [Cantor](heroes/cantor) did something he had not done in ninety years of singing under a window. He challenged the Court of Dusk in the Ring."], ["p", "The quarrel was an old one, of the kind the Ring was made for: a bell quarter of Aubade against a house of Gloaming. The bell quarter claimed one of its daughters, a singer of the hundred who had gone west in the first year and never come home; the Court said she was its harlequin, and free, and staying. Cantor stood for the bell quarter. He had told nobody why, after ninety years, that spring; he did not know himself. He was a hundred and twenty and looked forty-eight, and he thought, perhaps, that if he could not sing her home he could fight for her, and that she would have to look at him."], ["p", "The Court's champion was named on the morning of the duel, at Mum's own asking. It was Mum."], ["p", "They fought at noon on midsummer, at the Opening of the Ring, in front of forty thousand. [Referee Whistler](heroes/referee-whistler) called it. [Mr. Ledger](heroes/mr-ledger) wrote it. Cantor sang on the beat, as he always did, and his voice filled the stands, and Mum made no sound at all: her walls came up where nobody could see them, and her ball came out of nowhere, and the chalk lines she left on the sand drew the fight as it went, as if the world had to be drawn back in by hand. It was close. In the end Cantor walked into a wall that was not there, and sat down on the sand, and laughed, which nobody had heard him do since the Fall. The Arbiter raised the winner's arm: a pale glove, a white sleeve in ivory diamonds."], ["p", "It was noon on midsummer. The Tower's shadow lay across the Ring, as it always did, over Yoko's circle. Down that shadow, for the first time in ninety years, the Aumbry looked at the voice that had got away."], ["p", "It knew her. It had taken the choir in the hour of noon and missed her by a hair, and for ninety years she had lived a hair behind every hour there was, unknitted, out of its reach. It wanted her more than anything on the Dial, because she was the last who knew the verse that had made it let go of a sun, every midsummer, for four hundred years. It reached for her and could not hold her. So it took what it could hold: the hour of the duel. The duel, its winner, its record, everyone's memory of it."], ["p", "Forty thousand people blinked. The sand was raked. The sun was lower. Nobody could remember who had won, or quite why they were there. In the Tally-house, the page for the Opening's duel was blank, and Ledger, who had written it, checked the ink and found that the page had never been written on. The Arbiter remembered raising an arm and could not see the face; it was behind a porcelain mask, but he did not remember that either. Cantor remembered challenging. Mum remembered nothing."], ["p", "And on the Tower's grey face the gate, which had been shut since the Fall, stood open."], ["h", 2, "Who went in"], ["p", "It had opened for Mum. The Aumbry keeps what is sworn in its shadow, and the Truce had sworn that the gate opens only for a champion of the Ring; so it opened for the champion it wanted, and it did not close again, because now it wanted her to come, and a cupboard left open is how you catch a cat. Mum did not know it was for her. She went back to Gloaming. She did not go in."], ["p", "Others did. For a week the Truce argued about who had the right, and then the Arbiter ruled, standing under the cut letters on the gate: the clause said a champion of the Ring, and the Ring had a great many. Any champion who had won there might climb."], ["p", "The first through the gate was [Marin](heroes/marin). She had heard, from the Ring's stands, a sound coming out of it: the harbour bell of Sallowmouth, which she had last heard on the morning after the Fall, ringing the drowned home. Inside, the first floor of the Tower was a harbour at dawn, with the sea in its streets and still rising, and a woman in the sea temple's tower ringing the bell. The Bell-Warden did not know Marin. She did not know how long she had been ringing. Down on the quays the drowned dockers were working, and one of them was the boy who had given Marin a coral flower."], ["p", "At the end of the harbour, where the road went up toward a gate of grey stone, a woman stood holding the gate shut: a dancer of the sea temple, barefoot, in sea-glass, with the tide in ribbons in her hands. It was Marin. It was the Tower's echo of her, the one it had kept from her first duel in the Ring sixty years before, set up there as a keeper sets its best piece at the end of a room. Marin fought herself on the quay, and won, and the echo stood aside, and smiled at her with her own face."], ["p", "The same summer the Noonsworn chose a Bearer for the Ember of Aubade, to carry it up the Tower now that the Tower could be climbed: a broad, slow knight called the Sunbearer, who swore never to set it down except to fight. The same summer the Dawnless carried a speaker of the Greenwood Moot through the thickets by the Gloaming road and in at the open gate, and left a trail that a young hunter called the Huntmaster would follow for ten years and never tell the Moot about. And the Moss-Abbess, an old friend of the moth priestess walked in at the gate one evening to look for the chapel she had kept in the Wood's first year, and did not come out."], ["h", 2, "The Climb grows"], ["p", "The Tower showed its collection one room at a time, as climbers proved they could reach it."], ["p", "**The Sea**, floors 1 to 10, was Sallowmouth on the morning the water came, the shipyard and the drowned chapel and the lighthouse and the point of rock the gulls would not leave, ruled by the worst of that morning: the Drowned Admiral in the wreck of his flagship, who had ordered every ship to stay at anchor and went down with all of them; the Salt King, Mother of Wrecks, the Tide Judge, the Pearl Tyrant; the harbour's old cracked bell, which rings underwater; the Lantern Leviathan and the Gull Queen. At its tenth floor stood the Sun Warden himself, or the Tower's keeping of him, as he was at the moment it took the sun, gold and white with his great dial striking, who met each climber as if they were late, and fought them."], ["p", "**The Wood**, floors 11 to 20, opened in year 92. Its gate was held by the Tower's echo of [Fenna](heroes/fenna), and at its heart stood the Elder Root, a trunk too wide to see round, drinking from a root of the Tower's own: Fenna went in, and knew it for the oak she had planted at twelve, and it knew her. [Hank](heroes/hank) cut a piece of the root where it went up into the Tower's wall, and it bled sap that glowed, and the cut on his hand has not healed. Its rulers were the Moss Warden, the Thorn Mother, the Antler King, the Hollow Elder, the Briar Witch, the Lantern Root, the Grey Oak and the Mire Heart, and at its crown burned Ash and Ember, the sun's two last sparks, as the Tower kept them at the moment they fell. When Anka reached them, they looked at her a long time, and said, \"Not yet.\""], ["p", "**The Sands**, floors 21 to 30, opened in year 94: the Storm Canyon on the night of the great storm, its gate held by the echo of [Sefu](heroes/sefu) on his first night as king. The Scarab Colossus and her son the Scarab King, the Dune Colossus, the Brass Sphinx, the Glass Serpent, the Mirage Lord, the Sand Tyrant, the Red Jackal and the Sun-Eater ruled it, and at its crown was **the Heart of Noon**: the Tower's keeping of the sun's heart in the moment it fell, a sphere of white fire in a hall of black glass. It is the closest any climber has come to looking at what was taken."], ["p", "**The Deep**, floors 31 to 40, opened in year 96: the far halls on the night their crystals went out, with the Echo-Hermit listening to the rock and the gate held by the echo of [Opaline](heroes/opaline). The Prism Mother, the Hollow Queen, the Lamp Warden, the Glass Abbot, Mother of Echoes, the Seam King, the Blind Cantor, the Gem Tyrant and the Drowned Lantern ruled it. At its crown the Sun Warden waited a second time, and this time it was not the Tower's keeping of him. It was Iselm himself, come down from the Sun Court, because the word made him guard the Tower's crowns and he wished to see, with his own eyes, who was coming. He remembered every climber he had met on the tenth floor."], ["p", "**The War**, floors 41 to 50, opened in year 98: the Field of Banners on the night before the Ninth Field, with both armies on it and the Herald of both banners going from camp to camp trying to arrange a truce, and the Last Marshal guarding both. Its gate was held by the echo of the Unmaker, standing at the Field's edge where it had walked a hundred years before, because the war could not end while it stood there. The Iron Marshal, the Siege Lord, the Ashen General, the Banner Knight, the Crow King, the Black Herald, the War Drummer and the Grey Captain ruled it, and at its crown Ash and Ember burned again, under whatever was above. [Madame Cleaver](heroes/madame-cleaver) climbed to the forty-first floor, found a woman at a cauldron feeding both armies from one fire, and came down without a word. [Sir Whiskers](heroes/sir-whiskers) met a lynx knight on his last life in a helm he would not take off, and knew his brother's voice, and told nobody."], ["p", "Few climbers came down from above the twentieth floor. The Ringside said they had died. They had not, mostly. They had stood still somewhere, in a hall of drowned bells or a forest that would not stop walking, and grieved, and been kept."], ["h", 2, "The Hall of Noon"], ["p", "In year 93 the Underforge's drill, which [Brannoc](heroes/brannoc) had begun because he wanted the Heart's fire brought up through the rock, broke into a hall beneath the Ring's west tunnel, between a white court of fallen stone and a pit of embers, with the Hall of Noon under it all. The Truce closed it within the hour, and then, because it was a quarrel between the Underforge and everyone else, opened it to the Ring."], ["p", "What waited there was not the Tower's keeping of the sun's pieces but the pieces themselves, and they were not free. The Tower had bound them to guard the Heart it drank: a sentinel of the Sun Court made in the Warden's likeness, stone and gold round a small trapped sun; Ash and Ember up out of the Cinder Pit, two shapes of fire that fought whatever came near the Heart; and the Heart itself, which was no longer only a sun's heart but a furious, wounded thing that had been drunk from every morning for ninety years. The Truce named the place **Noonfall**, and let champions go down in pairs, with its leave, to fight them."], ["p", "The Hourkeepers measured what happened when they won. On the morning after a pair beat the Heart of Noon, the light that rose through the Tower was a little brighter, for a day, because for that day less of it was drunk. It was the first thing anyone had done in ninety years that made the morning better. The Ringside calls it **the Raid**, and the Truce keeps the count."], ["h", 2, "The Trials"], ["p", "In year 95 a second stair opened off the Ring's west tunnel, below the first. It led up, through a tower of rooms that were never the same two weeks running: halls of fire and halls of dark, crystals to break and floors to hold, and on every tenth room a hero of the Ring standing guard, drawn huge, with a boss's moves. Champions fought their way up it and came back down, and the next week it had changed. They called it **the Trials**, the weekly Tower."], ["p", "The heroes who guarded its rooms were the Tower's echoes of them, the ones it had kept from every duel since the Ring began. The Trials were the Aumbry measuring the Dial's champions, the way a collector weighs a piece in the hand before deciding where to put it. It was choosing which of them to keep."], ["h", 2, "The door under the sea temple"], ["p", "In year 97, at the Homecoming table in Tamerit, the Uncrowned struck her brother, and was sent to the Gaol of Oaths, where the Chainwarden chained her in the lowest cell. The Uncrowned was content there. She had nowhere to go, and nobody had believed her in ninety-seven years, and a cell is quiet."], ["p", "On the night of a spring tide in year 99 someone came to her through the old warden's secret door, from the other side: from the undercroft of the sea temple, and below it, from the root of the Tower that runs under the harbour. He was tall and old and wore gold and white, and he stooped under the low arch. He could not tell her what he was. He could not tell her what the Tower was, or what it meant to do. The word would not let him. But a door is only a door, and he could open one."], ["p", "\"It will be at noon,\" he said, \"on the hundredth midsummer. Tell them what you read.\""], ["p", "Then he went back down into the dark, and left the door open, and the Uncrowned walked out of the Gaol of Oaths through the temple's undercroft, leaving wet footprints on the floor, which a temple keeper called Gus found at dawn and mopped up before the Chainwarden came, without quite knowing why. [Marin](heroes/marin) found the floor still wet. The Chainwarden found the page in her grandmother's chain-book a month later. Wick knew the door was not one of his, and it frightened him more than anything since the Tear."], ["p", "The Uncrowned went south, to the Sands, and then west, and then nowhere, for a year, working up the courage to walk into the Ring, where everyone would be, and say a thing that nobody had ever believed."], ["p", "That year twenty new champions came to the Ring. One of them was a fox spirit who had been in the Tower. One was a ferryman of the dead whose rivers had begun to run uphill. One was the Uncrowned queen herself."]]}, "story/07-the-hundredth-summer": {"title": "7. The Hundredth Summer", "sub": "Part three, year 100: now. The Dial is told what the Tower is, and does not want to believe it, and then does.", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Signs"], ["p", "The hundredth summer came with everything at once."], ["p", "The Tear widened by a hand's breadth between the spring tide and the first warm week. The Hourkeepers' Slip-sheet showed days of thirty hours in the north. [Sefu](heroes/sefu) stood on the Glass Flats at the spring festival, as he had every spring for ninety-nine years, and for the first time the sand under his feet was colder than the air. [Anka](heroes/anka) burned in a duel at the Sandstone Canyon and took nearly a minute to rise. The coal in [Brannoc](heroes/brannoc)'s chest burned lower, and so did the Caldera, and he measured both and told nobody. The Restless got up in Gloaming in numbers [Mortis](heroes/mortis) had never recorded, and his curve and the Hourkeepers' paling, laid one over the other on his desk, were still the same line. The Walking Wood walked east faster than in any year since the Fall, toward the Tower, and the villages at its edge could not keep up. [Lyra](heroes/lyra)'s red star, which she had found at ten, was larger. The climbers who went up the Tower that spring came down fewer."], ["p", "And the light was thinner every morning, and nobody could say it was not."], ["p", "*That is where the Dial stands now. From here on, the Story tells what is still to come.*"], ["h", 2, "The Uncrowned at the Table"], ["p", "On the night after the spring's last duel, at the Truce Table in the hall behind the Ring, with winner and loser eating side by side and two hundred champions and officers on the benches, a woman in faded royal linen of wine and tarnished gold walked in from the dark and sat down at the end of the table."], ["p", "The Chainwarden was up before anyone else saw who it was, and the chains on her forearms were already loose. [Madame Cleaver](heroes/madame-cleaver) was quicker. She put a bowl of truce-pot in front of the Uncrowned queen and a spoon in her hand, and said, \"Eat,\" and the Uncrowned ate; and whoever has eaten at the Truce Table may not be struck in anger outside the Ring. The Chainwarden stood where she was with her chains in her hands and her jaw set, and the whole table waited."], ["p", "The Uncrowned finished the bowl. Then she stood up and told them what she had read on the wall of the first king's tomb a hundred and one years before."], ["p", "*The pin keeps suns. It kept one before ours. It will keep ours. Do not sing to it.*"], ["p", "She told them about the painting under it, a country of terraces under a red sun that did not move. She told them about the man in gold and white who had come through the Gaol's secret door on the night of the spring tide, and what he had said: *it will be at noon, on the hundredth midsummer.* She said that the Tower had taken the sun, that it was a thing that kept, that the floors of the Climb were not copies but the realms' own lost hours with their lost people in them, and that at noon on the Hundredth Midsummer it would take whatever was left."], ["p", "Then she sat down again, and the table laughed."], ["p", "Not all of it. Sefu did not laugh; he was white with rage, because his sister had said in front of the whole Ring that the Father had not come home to the Sands but been stolen, and that she had known for a hundred years. [Auriel](heroes/auriel) did not laugh, because she had been raised on the Hymn. The Countess [Vesper](heroes/vesper) did not laugh, because she was watching who did."], ["p", "[Wick](heroes/wick) stood up. He was a hundred and fifty years old by the count, and looked seventy, and he had never in a hundred years spoken at the Truce Table. He told them about the hundredth door, and the Ring on the other side of it, at night, empty, a hundred years from the night he opened it. He had worked it out years ago: that night was four summers away. Whatever emptied the Ring would have done it by then."], ["p", "The table stopped laughing."], ["h", 2, "The pieces on the table"], ["p", "What came out that night, one champion at a time, was a hundred years of things each of them had kept to themselves."], ["p", "[Bram](heroes/bram) set Gerald on the table and showed them the four words cut into the limestone under the carved face, which nobody had ever read, and said there were nine stones like him round the Tower's foot, and he had found three. The fox spirit said she had seen that script before: on the lanterns of the revel she had danced at, three hundred years ago, in the Tower, which she had fallen out of on the morning after the Fall. The Great Zambini, who had never once told the Ringside where he came from, took off his hat, and said quietly that it was true, and that he had come out of the thirty-first door in year 40, and that everyone he had ever loved was still behind it, dancing. The Golemwright said the old word on the wall of the cave by the black water, where his first golem had walked into the dark, had been in that script, and that it was gone now, and his own name was cut there in its place."], ["p", "[Tempo](heroes/tempo) read them a line from the Hourkeepers' Noon Folio, which had never been read outside Frostfall: *it is dying, and something hastens it.* He told them he had seen himself on the War floors of the Climb, a heartbeat ahead, going up. [Barry Cloudsley](heroes/barry-cloudsley) said, for once without blaming Kaia, that he believed the Tower was taking the weather into itself, floor by floor. [Mortis](heroes/mortis) laid out his curve. [Grandma Ironknit](heroes/grandma-ironknit) said she had looked for the Tower's thread in the cloth of the Dial for three hundred years and never found it, and that she had come to think it was not in the cloth, but was the cloth. [Mr. Ledger](heroes/mr-ledger) closed his red book and said, in the voice he used for debtors, that if half of this was true, the Tower had taken a sun, a choir, a market, nine realms' hours and every climber who had not come down, and had never once paid for any of it."], ["p", "Then [Mum](heroes/mum) got up from her place among the Court of Dusk, and walked out of the hall, and nobody stopped her, because nobody ever did."], ["h", 2, "The page"], ["p", "She came back with the Tally."], ["p", "Ledger let her take it; he did not know why. She set it on the Truce Table among the bowls and opened it at the page for the Opening's duel of year 90, the blank page, the one Shiori's ink would not take on. Then she took a stick of chalk from her sleeve, the chalk she draws her walls with in the Ring, and wrote on it."], ["p", "The chalk took."], ["p", "What she wrote is still on the page: *Cantor of Aubade, for the bell quarter, against Mum of the Court of Dusk. Mum. A wall.* As she wrote it the hour came back to everyone in the hall who had been in the stands that day, all at once, like a word on the tip of the tongue: the noon, the forty thousand, the voice filling the stands, the man who walked into a wall that was not there and sat down on the sand and laughed. The Arbiter saw the face behind the arm he had raised. It was a porcelain mask with a crack behind the left eye."], ["p", "Cantor sat very still. He had fought her, and lost, and laughed, and had not remembered for ten years."], ["p", "Mum wrote one more line, under the first, on the Tally's page, where nobody can rub it out. She has not written anything else on it since."], ["p", "*It opened for me. It wants what I know.*"], ["p", "[Cantor](heroes/cantor) understood first. Mum knew the Hymn's last verse. The master had taught it to her the week before the Fall, in case anything happened to him, and something had. He had always thought she was afraid of the verse because she had failed the Hymn. He saw now that she was afraid of it because it mattered, and that the Tower had opened its gate for her, and left it open for ten years, the way you leave a door open for a cat."], ["h", 2, "The council"], ["p", "The Truce Table sat until the slip made it the next day."], ["p", "Every faction said what it had always said, and every one of them heard, for the first time, how it sounded against what the Uncrowned had read. The Noonsworn would carry the Ember of Aubade to the Noon Seat and hang the sun again. The Court of Dusk would keep the Seat empty, until Vesper learned from the fox spirit that the Tower held Gloaming's Long Dusk among its keepings, the year without a day, with the sick in their beds and the vintners in their cellars, and that her steward's daughter, who had gone to the midsummer market, was kept with the market; then the Countess said nothing at all for a long time. The Dune Throne wanted the Seat for its king, and its king, for the first time in a hundred years, did not say so. The Underforge wanted the Heart left below. The Moot wanted the roots cut. The Windward wanted whatever worked. The Hourkeepers wanted to understand it first, and [Shiori](heroes/shiori) said, gently, that they had had three hundred years."], ["p", "It was the Arbiter who settled it, because that is what he is for. [Referee Whistler](heroes/referee-whistler) stood up on the bench and read them the Truce."], ["p", "The first clause: no realm makes war over the sun again. So no realm would go up alone. The fourth: the Tower's gate opens only for a champion of the Ring. So whoever went would go as champions, under the Truce, with every faction in the company and none of them in charge. The fifth: every duel is written in the Tally, and every debt it settles is paid. The Tower had sat in the Truce's shadow for a hundred years, he said, and kept the Truce's fourth clause cut in its own gate, and it was bound by the others as much as by that one. It owed. The Truce would go up and present the bill."], ["p", "\"A challenge isn't real,\" said [Pip](heroes/pip), from the far end of the table, \"until it's delivered by hand.\" And Ledger began, without being asked, to write it."], ["h", 2, "What they knew, and what they did not"], ["p", "They did not know yet what the Tower was, or where it came from, or what its word was. They did not know that the man in gold and white at the top had made it. They knew enough."], ["p", "They knew that every made thing has a word, because the Golemwright had made a hundred golems and each obeyed whoever had written the last word on its brow. They knew Shiori's writing comes true, a little, for a while, and once for ever. They knew that the fox spirit can steal a word out of the air, and had once stolen her own true name off a golem's brow, where someone who should not have known it had written it. They knew Mum held a verse the Tower wanted. They knew that the Obsidian Herald of the Underforge carried a word the Caldera's fire had given her at fifteen, which she believed was the sun's own name and had never said aloud; and that the fire, she said that night, was waiting for someone to come down to it, and had told her who. She looked down the table at the Sunbearer with the Ember of Aubade in its brass shrine on his back. The Noonsworn had always said the Ember went up."], ["p", "And they knew they had until noon on the Hundredth Midsummer, which was nine weeks away."], ["h", 2, "The company"], ["p", "It went up in the second week of the summer, through the gate, with the Arbiter's horn and forty thousand people in the stands who had come to see the Ring and stayed to watch the gate."], ["p", "It was not an army. The Truce allowed none. It was a company of champions, one at least from every faction, and some who belonged to none: Mum, for whom the gate had opened; Cantor, who would not let her go up without him a second time; Auriel of the Noonsworn, whose father had been a tenor of the hundred; the Countess Vesper, who wanted to see what was in the Seat before anyone else did, and [Nyx](heroes/nyx) in her shadow; Sefu, without his crown's guard, and his sister the Uncrowned, whom he would not look at; Shiori, with her brush and the Hourkeepers' blank books, to write down everything; Tempo, to find himself; The Great Zambini and the fox spirit, who knew the Tower from the inside; [Sir Whiskers](heroes/sir-whiskers), for his brother; Anka, to find the rest of her fire; and the Sky-Stitcher, who meant to sew the Tear shut from close to. At the gate, two Briarkin children who looked ten were found to be inside Shiori's book-pack, and were not sent home, because by the time anyone noticed they were on the second floor."], ["p", "The rest of the Dial did its own part. Each chapter's heroes were waiting at their chapter's gate: the Windward at the Sea, the Moot at the Wood, the Throne's court at the Sands, the Underforge at the Deep, and at the War, a cook. Below the Ring the Raid's pairs went down to Noonfall, with the Sunbearer among them, carrying the Ember the other way. And the Truce's own officers stayed at the Ring, to hold it: the Arbiter, the Tithe-Keeper, the Doorwarden, the Saltwarden, the Chainwarden, the Feastmistress and the Mountain, who had not stepped out of his circle in a hundred years and was not going to start now."]]}, "story/08-the-sea-and-the-wood": {"title": "8. The Sea and the Wood", "sub": "Part four, the Climb, floors 1 to 20. A drowned harbour that is still drowning, and a forest in its first year of walking.", "blocks": [["h", 2, "The harbour at dawn"], ["p", "The first floor of the Tower is a morning a hundred years old."], ["p", "The company came through the gate into grey light and the smell of salt, onto a quay above a harbour full of wrecks. The sea was in the streets of Sallowmouth and still rising. Lanterns burned on the docks. Gulls wheeled over everything. Every seventy-eight seconds the tide came in, fifteen seconds of it, and the tidal flats flooded and the company ran for the high ground, and then it went out again, and the harbour bell rang. [Shiori](heroes/shiori) timed it, and wrote down that it was the only thing in the Tower that kept perfect time."], ["p", "The bell was rung by a woman in the sea temple's tower, the **Bell-Warden**, who had rung it all night to call the boats home, and who did not know that the night had been a hundred years long. She came down to the quay to meet them, because strangers on the first floor were rare and she was a polite woman. She asked if they had seen her husband's boat. They had not. A man called Tam, a net-mender, had lost his way between the net sheds and the chapel, and the **Net-Father**, who mended the harbour's nets on the quay as if nothing had happened, asked them to lead him home. [Pim and Pom](heroes/pim-and-pom) did, by tagging each other down every alley until one of them found his door. The **Lamp-Bearer**, out on the shore road, kept the shore lamps lit for the boats, and the lamps kept going out."], ["p", "[Gus](heroes/gus) had come up with them as far as the first floor, with his broom. He had been here once before and seen the sea temple as it was on that morning: filthy, flooding, and full of frightened people. He went straight to it. The company left him there, sweeping the water off the temple floor toward the door, and telling the frightened people, in his calm way, to mind where they stepped, and leading them, one by one, up out of the water to the temple's high floor, where it was dry. Nobody had done that for them in a hundred years. When the company looked back from the harbour road the temple's lamps were lit, and its floor was wet, which, as Gus says, is how you know it is clean."], ["h", 2, "The Tide Dancer at the gate"], ["p", "At the end of the harbour, where the road went up toward a gate of grey stone, the Tower's echo of [Marin](heroes/marin) held the gate shut, as it had for ten years: a barefoot girl in sea-glass with the tide in ribbons in her hands, kept from her first duel in the Ring sixty years before."], ["p", "The real Marin had come up with them for this. She had fought her echo many times, and won, and the echo had always stood aside and smiled. This time she did not fight. She stood in front of it on the quay and danced, and the tide came in, and went out, in her hands and in its hands at once, the same tide, and the echo, which was only a keeping of her at one hour, could not keep up with her at all the hours since. It stopped. It looked at her with her own face and asked, in her own voice, the one thing the Tower had kept it wondering for sixty years:"], ["p", "\"Are you still dancing?\""], ["p", "\"Yes,\" said Marin. \"I never stopped. The sea wouldn't let me.\""], ["p", "The echo stepped aside. Down on the quays, the drowned dockers had stopped working to watch her, as they always did when she danced, and one of them, a boy with a coral flower behind his ear, lifted his hand."], ["h", 2, "The drowned and their masters"], ["p", "The rest of the Sea was the same harbour, rearranged and harder each floor, and the worst of that morning in each."], ["p", "On the first and second floors the **Drowned Admiral** fought them from the wreck of his flagship across the harbour mouth, with anchor and cannon and the tide. He had ordered every ship to stay at anchor to ride out the strange tide, and gone down with all of them, and he still believed he had given the right order. [Sir Whiskers](heroes/sir-whiskers) duelled him on the wreck's tilted deck, and lost a cuff and kept his life, and told him, as the Admiral went down under his own anchor, that the right order is the one you can still give the morning after. The **Salt King** of the third floor weighed them and found them wanting. **Mother of Wrecks** lit false lights on the headland of the fourth. On the fifth the **Tide Judge** set the tide as their sentence, and [Auriel](heroes/auriel) broke it with her shields. The **Pearl Tyrant** of the sixth sent his pale diver after them. On the seventh floor was **the Bell Below**: the harbour's old cracked bell, thrown into the sea when the new one was cast, which rings underwater, and something answers. The something was the new bell, the Bell-Warden's, ringing back. The Bellwright, who had cast them both, had come up to this floor once before and wept on the quay; this time she swam down, and put her bronze-stained hands on the old bell, and struck it once with her own hammer, on its true note, and it stopped answering, and hung still in the green water, and she said she would bring it home when the Tower let it go. On the eighth the **Lantern Leviathan** came up out of the deep sea with its lantern burning, and on the ninth the **Gull Queen**, who had fed the gulls from her hand for forty years, and whom they would not leave."], ["p", "Each ruler, when it fell, said the same thing, in its own way. *It was the worst morning. Somebody had to keep it.*"], ["h", 2, "The Warden as he was"], ["p", "At the tenth floor, the Sea's crown, on a court of white stone above the harbour with a great dial set into the floor, a man in gold and white was on one knee with both hands flat on the stone."], ["p", "It was the Sun Warden, or the Tower's keeping of him, as he was in the instant it took the sun: struck from inside, kneeling at his dial. When they came near he stood up, and received them as if they were late, and fought them, and he was very strong, because the moment it had kept was the moment the Aumbry had been strongest. The dial struck every few heartbeats, and where it struck, the floor burned gold. It took the whole company to bring him down, and [Anka](heroes/anka) burned, and rose, slowly."], ["p", "When he went down on one knee again, as he had been, his dial struck nine times, and stopped, and he spoke, in a tongue none of them knew, one line. [Mum](heroes/mum) knew it. She stood quite still. It was the first line of the last verse."], ["p", "[Cantor](heroes/cantor) looked at her and saw her lips move. No sound came out. She shut her eyes."], ["h", 2, "The Wood walking"], ["p", "The eleventh floor was the Walking Wood in its first year: trees that did not stand still, paths that moved while you were on them, a moss chapel at the forest's edge with its door open, and a great dark heart to the forest that was not dark, because something at its centre glowed."], ["p", "[Fenna](heroes/fenna) and [Hank](heroes/hank) were waiting at the Wood's gate, where the Tower's echo of Fenna held it. Fenna did not fight her echo. She planted a flower at its feet, and stood among the flowers with it, and they grew, and the echo, which had only the strength of one hour, had no flowers of its own; it stood aside. Then the two of them led the company to the forest's heart."], ["p", "The **Elder Root** stood there, a trunk too wide to see round, roots that arched over the paths like bridges, a core that glowed, and three great roots that fed it, running out through the soil to the edge of the floor and into the Tower's wall. It was the oak Fenna had planted at twelve, which had walked away with the Wood on the day of the Fall. The Tower had drawn the whole Wood toward itself through its roots, and drawn this tree furthest, into the forest's heart, and set it to drink from the Tower's own root, as a cutting is set in water. Fenna had stood in front of it before and known it, and it had known her. Now she put her hand on its bark and asked it, aloud, to let go."], ["p", "It could not. It was kept. Hank cut the three roots that fed it, one after another, with the great green-edged axe, while the bark hounds came out of the dark for him and the company held them off; the third root, where it went into the wall, was the one he had cut ten years before, and the cut on his hand opened again and bled the same glowing sap. When the third root parted the Elder Root shuddered from crown to foot, and its core dimmed, and the paths of the forest, for a moment, stood still."], ["p", "\"Not yet,\" said Fenna, to the tree, and to Hank. \"But it will.\""], ["h", 2, "The two abbesses"], ["p", "The keeper of the Wood's ten floors is the **Moss-Abbess**, who keeps the moss chapel at the forest's edge and sounds a horn at dusk to help the Wood sleep. There were two of them."], ["p", "One was a woman of forty in a moss-green habit, with a horn at her belt, keeping the chapel in the Wood's first year as if it were the only year there had ever been. The other was an old woman of the same face, in a faded habit of the same green, who sat beside her on the chapel step and helped her with the horn. The old one had walked in at the gate in year 90, looking for the chapel she had kept long ago, and found it, and found herself in it, kept, and sat down beside herself. She had been helping herself keep the paths for ten years. She did not want to go home. She was not unhappy. She had simply stopped."], ["p", "The moth priestess had come up the Wood's floors to find her. She had dreamed the Abbess was alive, and she was. The moth priestess sat on the step between the two of them, the young one and the old one, and folded her pale green wings round them both, and did not lay them to sleep. She told the old Abbess what the Wood was doing outside: walking east faster than ever, toward the Tower, because the Tower was pulling it, and the villages at its edge could not keep up. The old Abbess listened. At the end she stood up, slowly, and said she supposed she had better come and help, and took her horn, and came. The young Abbess stayed, and sounded hers at dusk, alone."], ["p", "It was the first time anyone kept by the Tower had chosen to leave it. Shiori wrote it down. It was not the last."], ["h", 2, "Thorns and the lost"], ["p", "The Wood's rulers were what the Wood had been worst at in its first year. The **Moss Warden**, the Moot's first warden, guarded it from everyone, including the folk. The **Thorn Mother** wove thorn hedges round everything. The **Antler King** led the bark hounds in the hunt. The **Hollow Elder** carried a light inside it that was not a fire. The **Briar Witch** had sold curses at the Wood's edge and been taken by her own briars. The **Lantern Root** lured the lost with an amber light, the **Grey Oak** would not fall, and the **Mire Heart** swallowed what walked across it."], ["p", "Among the thorn sprites, the faded Briarkin who half came back inside the Tower because nobody remembered them well enough to bring them back whole, was the greatest of them, **Grandmother Thorn**, who remembered being of the Spindle court and hated Grandma Ironknit for being remembered. She came for Pim and Pom in a hedge of thorns. The twins stopped running for once and told her, together, that they were Ironknit's, and that Ironknit had dropped three stitches at the Fall and looked for them in the cloth every night for a hundred years, and that she would look for Grandmother Thorn too, if they told her the name. Grandmother Thorn told them. The thorns let them through."], ["p", "On the Wood's floors the Huntmaster, who leads the Hunt of the Moot, found at last the end of a trail he had followed for ten years: a Moot speaker, the one killed by the Gloaming road in year 90, sitting under a hive-tree in the Wood's first year, alive, kept, puzzled. Beside the speaker's footprints in the soft ground were other prints, long and flat, that left no weight: a shadow's. The Huntmaster had always known they were not Nyx's. [Nyx](heroes/nyx), who had come up in the Countess's shadow, crouched beside him in the dark and looked at them a long time, and said, \"The Dawnless,\" which was the first word she had ever spoken to him. They hunted it together after that."], ["h", 2, "The sparks"], ["p", "At the twentieth floor, the Wood's crown, in a clearing the trees would not go near, burned **Ash and Ember**: the sun's two last sparks, as the Tower had kept them at the instant they fell, one a hunched brute of basalt and magma on his knuckles, one a flame in a burnt mantle with two lanterns orbiting her. The Wood feared fire above everything, and the trees round the clearing leaned away."], ["p", "They fought, because the Tower had set them there and they could not do otherwise. When they were down, they did not go out. They looked at [Anka](heroes/anka), as they had looked at her once before, and she asked them what she had asked them then: whether an Ember could choose to go home."], ["p", "\"Not yet,\" they said, as before. And then, because she had come further this time, the flame in the mantle said more."], ["p", "\"When the hand opens.\""]]}, "story/09-the-sands-and-the-deep": {"title": "9. The Sands and the Deep", "sub": "Part four, the Climb, floors 21 to 40. A king meets the night he was crowned, a sun's heart is weighed, and the Tower's keeper shows what it is.", "blocks": [["h", 2, "The night of the storm"], ["p", "The twenty-first floor was the Storm Canyon in the Sands on the night of the great storm of year 1: red walls, sand in the air thick enough to chew, a beacon tower on the rim, caravan towns half buried, raiders crouched behind their shields waiting for a caravan that would never come. The keeper of the Sands' floors is the **Star-Reader**, who climbs the beacon each night to read the sky through the storm and has never once seen a star. The **Storm-Counter** counts the lightning and the **Beacon-Keeper** keeps the fire lit."], ["p", "The gate was held by the Tower's echo of [Sefu](heroes/sefu) on the night it took the canyon: twenty-four, the crown new on his head, the sandstone crook in his hand, and afraid."], ["p", "Sefu went to meet it himself. He was a hundred and twenty-four and looked forty-four, and he had worn the cursed crown for ninety-nine years and outlasted everyone else it passed to, because his embalmer brewed him a draught each night that kept it from taking him and shortened his life by a year for each month. He stood in front of himself in the storm, and the echo raised the crook to Judge him, and Sefu did not raise his."], ["p", "\"I did not take it,\" he said, to the boy with his face. He said it loud enough for the whole company to hear, and for his sister. \"They offered it at midnight, and I was afraid of what they would do if I said no. I have said for a hundred years that I took it.\""], ["p", "The echo lowered the crook. It had been kept at the hour of a lie, and the lie was over. It stood aside. The Uncrowned had heard. She did not say anything. But she walked beside her brother through the storm after that, and he let her."], ["h", 2, "The worst of the storm"], ["p", "The Sands' rulers were old, and some of them were older than the Fall. The **Scarab Colossus** of the twenty-first floor, a beetle the size of a house that the storm had uncovered under the canyon, burrowed and marked the sand and called the storm; the Dune folk say it rolled the sun across the sky once, and the mother of a god had a right to be angry that the sun was gone. Her son, the gilded **Scarab King**, worshipped by the raiders, ruled the twenty-second. The **Dune Colossus** walked with the dunes. The **Brass Sphinx** of the twenty-fourth asked her riddle, which nobody had ever answered: *what holds the noon and has no hands?* The Uncrowned answered it. \"The pin,\" she said, \"and it has one.\" The Sphinx was so surprised she forgot to fight."], ["p", "The **Glass Serpent**, born the day the sun's heat struck the sand, ruled the twenty-fifth. On the twenty-sixth was the **Mirage Lord**, a prince of the Throne who had walked onto the Glass Flats to be alone and become a mirage of himself; the mirage archer, whose grandmother's brother he was, made him a mirage in return, of a road home across the Flats with a lamp at the end of it, and the Mirage Lord walked onto it, and lay down in the shade of a palm that was not there, and was quiet. The **Sand Tyrant**, who had sold water by the cup in a drought, the **Red Jackal**, who had ridden for the crimson, and the **Sun-Eater**, a priest of the Father who had tried to swallow a piece of the fallen sun to become it and burned from inside, ruled the floors above. The Lion of the Gate, on the stone lion that is not stone, carried the company through the worst of the storm. The Throne's embalmer, [Vitriol](heroes/vitriol), took samples of everything, in small glass vials, and labelled them, and was happier than anyone had seen him in two hundred years."], ["h", 2, "The Father's heart"], ["p", "At the thirtieth floor, in a hall of black glass, hung **the Heart of Noon**: the Tower's keeping of the sun's heart in the instant it fell, a sphere of white fire whose light was the day. It was the closest any of them had come to the thing that had been taken."], ["p", "It fought them, as the crowns do. It did not want to; it could not be other than it had been in that instant, which was falling, and furious, and afraid. When it was brought down it hung low over the black glass floor, pulsing, and the hall was very quiet."], ["p", "The Throne's judge of the dead, the Heartweigher, set her scale before it. In the pan she laid the feather she had weighed every king's heart against, which they say is one of Anka's, taken from the sea on the morning after the Fall. In the other pan she laid nothing, because nobody can lift a sun's heart into a scale; she held the pan up to its light instead, and the light weighed it. It sank."], ["p", "\"Heavier,\" The Heartweigher said, as the office requires. Then, which the office does not allow, she said why. \"With grief. Not with sin.\""], ["p", "Sefu had come up the Tower to sit in the Noon Seat and become the new sun, because the Father had come home to his children and the crown of the Sands was the Father's crown. He looked at the heart of the thing his line had called father for six hundred years, weighed, and wounded, and heavier with grief than any king, and he understood that it had not come home to anyone. It had been taken, and was trying to get back. He did not say anything for a whole floor. On the thirty-first he gave the crook to his sister to carry, without a word, and she carried it."], ["p", "From the tombs of the Throne, the veiled priestess had come up to look at the Heart with her veil lifted, to see whether a sun could be turned to stone. She lifted the veil. The Heart did not turn to stone. It looked back at her, the way the sun looks back at a face turned up to it, and the marble vein in her throat that her own gaze had left there years ago went warm, and she put the veil down and wept, and would not say why."], ["h", 2, "The night the crystals went out"], ["p", "The thirty-first floor was the far halls of the Deep on the night their crystals went out, with the miners standing in the dark where the light had left them, and a hermit listening to the rock. The **Echo-Hermit** keeps the Deep's ten floors. He has listened to the rock so long that the rock has started to answer, and the company found him with his ear to a wall, saying that there was something on the other side digging up."], ["p", "The gate was held by the Tower's echo of [Opaline](heroes/opaline), with a ring of prisms round her head. The real Opaline was waiting for them in the dark, and so, to everyone's surprise, was her sister. [Silvane](heroes/silvane) had come up the Deep's floors with a mirror in her hand, the hand mirror in which she had caught the sun's last light at twelve and never let it out. The sisters had not spoken in sixty years. They did not speak now. Opaline broke the Deep's light into its colours against her echo, and Silvane turned the colours back with her mirrors, and between them they made a light the echo had not been kept with, and it stood aside. Then Opaline said, \"You've been watching me through a mirror for sixty years,\" and Silvane said, \"Yes,\" and that was the end of it, and they walked on together."], ["p", "[Mabel](heroes/mabel)'s lantern crystal, which still holds the first light of the Shining, sang its high note all the way through the far halls. The Hallbreaker closed three galleries on the lurkers coming up out of the deep rock, from the tunnel Mabel had once found and filled in, a tunnel dug from below: it was one of the Tower's roots, pushing up. The Golemwright heard his first golem through the rock, the one that had walked into the black water with no word on its brow, still walking, and walking up."], ["p", "The Deep's rulers: the **Prism Mother**, a crystal the size of a hall that turned its beams in the dark to find the others; the **Hollow Queen**, an Old Mother of the mole-folk who dug too deep alone; the **Lamp Warden**, who swore the lamps would never go out and went out with them; the **Glass Abbot**, who cut his own eyes into crystal; **Mother of Echoes**, who answers every step a third time; the **Seam King**, who worked his miners in the dark; the **Gem Tyrant**, buried by the light he hoarded; and the **Drowned Lantern**, a lamp that fell into the black water and kept burning under it. And one more, on the thirty-seventh floor."], ["h", 2, "The Blind Cantor"], ["p", "He sat on a ledge above the black water in the white and gold of the hundred, a big man, a bass, with his eyes burned white, singing the Hymn of Noon to the dark on the beat, as he had been singing it for a hundred years. He had been taken with the choir in the hour of noon; the Tower had filed him here, in the dark, because the sun's cracking had blinded him in the instant it took him, and a blind singer belonged, to the Aumbry's way of keeping, with the night the light went out. The climbers had called him a ruler, because he would not let them pass his ledge while the Hymn was being sung."], ["p", "[Cantor](heroes/cantor) knew his voice in the first bar. It was the bass who had sat across from him in the Sun Court for ten years."], ["p", "Cantor did not fight him. He sat down on the ledge beside him and sang the Hymn with him, on the beat, the two voices together for the first time since the Fall, and when they came to the end of the Hymn proper, where the last verse begins, the Blind Cantor stopped, as he always stopped, because only the master knew it."], ["p", "\"Who was behind the beat?\" Cantor asked him. \"On the day. I have always believed it was Ansel.\""], ["p", "The Blind Cantor laughed, a bass laugh, a hundred years old. \"Ansel? Ansel was the steadiest tenor in the hundred. It was the girl, at the master's right hand. A hair behind, in the second line, and you turned your head toward her, I saw you. And then the master was gone, before he could breathe for the verse, and then we all were. Everyone who was on the beat.\" He turned his white eyes toward Cantor. \"She did not drop it, brother. It took us because we were in step. It missed her because she wasn't. And it missed you because you were looking at her.\""], ["p", "Cantor went back down the stair to the company and found Mum, and sat by her, and told her, in front of everyone, what the Blind Cantor had said. She listened. Then, for the first time in a hundred years, she put her hand on his."], ["p", "Auriel had heard too. Her father had been the steadiest tenor in the hundred."], ["h", 2, "The keeper himself"], ["p", "At the fortieth floor, in a cold white court like the one at the Sea's crown, the Sun Warden was waiting again, and he was not on his knees."], ["p", "He was himself: Iselm, who had come down from the Sun Court at the top because the word made him guard the Tower's crowns, and because he had wanted, for a hundred years, to see who would come. He remembered every climber he had met on the tenth floor, though it had not been him there. He fought them because he had to, and he fought them hard, because he had to know whether they were enough. They were."], ["p", "When it was over he sat down on the edge of his dial, an old man in gold and white, and [Shiori](heroes/shiori) opened her book."], ["p", "\"I cannot tell you what it is,\" he said. \"The word will not let me tell anyone. It does not forbid me to walk with a guest.\""], ["p", "He walked them up one stair further, to a window, as he had walked the first king of the Sands six hundred years before. Through it they saw a country of terraces round a valley, white stone and blue glass, under a great red sun that did not move, and people on every terrace standing still and glad in the noon light. At a high window of a pale tower in the middle of it a girl of nine sat up in bed with the sun on her face, as if she were halfway through a question."], ["p", "He could not tell them, but they could ask, and he would not lie: when the answer was no, he said no. So they asked, and Shiori wrote down every question, and he did not say no once. Whether the Tower had been made. Whether he had made it, which took him a long time not to answer. Whether that was his world. Whether the girl at the window was his. Whether it kept what was ending. Whether, to it, everything was. Whether it had taken their sun. Whether it would take the rest at noon on midsummer. Whether it would leave, after. Whether the Unmaker was what was left of that world, behind it."], ["p", "\"What is its word?\" asked the Golemwright, who had come up with the Underforge's party to the Deep's crown and would go no higher. \"Every made thing has one.\""], ["p", "The Warden looked at him for a long time, and then smiled, which none of them had seen him do. He could not say the word, because the word would not let him speak against itself. But he could say his own name, and he did."], ["p", "\"Iselm,\" he said. \"In my tongue it means *keep*.\""], ["p", "The fox spirit heard it the way she hears every spell said aloud near her: as a thing she could take. \"Then it's your name,\" she said. \"Its word is your name. I could steal it.\""], ["p", "\"Then I would be nothing,\" said Iselm."], ["p", "\"Would you mind?\""], ["p", "He thought about it, as a careful man does. \"No,\" he said. \"Not if something else were written in its place.\""], ["p", "Before they went on, Shiori asked him to teach her to write it: not *keep*, which she would not write, but the way his people wrote. He taught her the script of Senmere on the white floor of the fortieth floor, with her brush, until the slip made it the next day. The Golemwright watched, and learned it too, and went down. Mum asked him nothing at all. When the company climbed on, he did not go with them. He went up the other stair, the one only he used, to the Sun Court, to be at his dial when they came."]]}, "story/10-the-war": {"title": "10. The War", "sub": "Part four, the Climb, floors 41 to 50. A war that cannot end, a cook, a horn, and the first of the Tower's hours to be let go.", "blocks": [["h", 2, "The night before the battle"], ["p", "The forty-first floor is the Field of Banners on the last night of the war, in the spring of year 2: the siege line, the burned farm, the field hospital, the old fort and the ruined keep, signal fires on the ridges, and two armies drawn up in the mud for a battle that has not been fought. Crimson and azure footmen, bowmen and knights skirmish across it all night, and the ashen soldiers, the dead of both banners from two years of war, fight beside them. Arrows fall on a line. Nobody inside has told them the war is over, and they would not believe it, because for them the Ninth Field is tomorrow."], ["p", "The keeper of the War's floors is **the Herald**, who speaks for both banners and goes from camp to camp with a white staff to arrange a truce, and has been going for a hundred years; nobody has listened yet. The **Turncloak** keeps the camp, a lynx knight of the Ninefold on his last life who will not take his helm off. The **White Sister**, born in Lowlight in Aubade, mends the wounded of both sides in the field hospital. The **Camp-Mother** feeds both armies from one fire on the odd floors, and says she learned the trick from a cook called Cleaver. The **Quartermaster** sells the stores on the even floors to whoever pays."], ["p", "The gate was held by the Unmaker. Not the Tower's echo of it this time, though the echo was there: the Unmaker itself had come."], ["h", 2, "The Unmaker at the edge of the Field"], ["p", "[Oblivion](heroes/oblivion) had climbed alone, from the gate, floor by floor, a day behind the company, as it had climbed before. It had never fought its way past the War's gate. The thing that held the gate was itself, the Tower's keeping of it as it was on the night it walked across the Field, curious, not yet knowing what it was; and it could not unmake itself."], ["p", "It knew now. The company had left word for it on the white floor of the fortieth, in Shiori's hand, where it would pass. It said what Iselm had shown them: that the Unmaker was the evening of a world called Senmere, the ending that world was never allowed to have, dragged behind the Tower from one sky to the next; that it had been pulled through the Tear at noon by the taking, as it had always believed; and that it wanted the Dial unmade only because it was itself an ending with nothing to end."], ["p", "Oblivion read it twice. Then it walked up to its own echo at the Field's edge, the shape of black cracked with pink in the form of an armoured man on a terrace of Senmere, and said the thing it had been trying to say for a hundred years and had not had the words for."], ["p", "\"There was something here,\" it said. \"There was.\""], ["p", "The echo, which had been kept at an hour when it did not yet know, looked at the Unmaker as it was now, and knew. It came apart like smoke, and the gate of the War stood open, and the Unmaker walked through it, and caught up with the company it had fought for a hundred years, and walked beside it."], ["h", 2, "The cook comes back"], ["p", "[Madame Cleaver](heroes/madame-cleaver) had climbed to the forty-first floor once before, found a woman at a cauldron feeding both armies from one fire, and come down without a word. She had never told anyone why. It was not the Camp-Mother she could not face. It was the drummer."], ["p", "On the first night of the Winter of Mud she had caught a starving azure boy stealing from her pot, and fed him. He was the drummer of the azure, and on the last night of the war the Tower had taken him with the rest, and he was here, on the forty-eighth floor, a boy still, beating the advance on his drum; and when he beat it the ashen dead got up and marched. Climbers called him the **War Drummer** and fought their way past him. Cleaver had never been able to climb that far."], ["p", "This time she came up with her cauldron on her back, with the company, and she did not turn back at the forty-first. She found the Camp-Mother at her fire in the ruined orchard, a woman of her own age, as she had been, with her own trick. The two of them looked at each other for a while. Then Cleaver hung her cauldron beside the Camp-Mother's, and lit it, and they cooked."], ["p", "It had never worked in a hundred years, the Camp-Mother said. They came, both banners, and ate, and went back and fought."], ["p", "\"It needs a Truce,\" said Cleaver. \"I didn't have one either, the first time. It took a boy with a slate.\""], ["h", 2, "The horn"], ["p", "The Truce was coming up the stairs behind them, two at a time."], ["p", "[Pip](heroes/pip) the courier had left the Ring at dawn with the Truce's challenge to the Tower in his pack, sealed in black and ivory, and a second thing the Arbiter had lent him, which no courier had ever carried: the silver horn on its gold chain, the one sound both armies of the Banners still stop for. The Tithe-Keeper had written the challenge; the Arbiter had signed it; the Truce Table had witnessed it; and a challenge is not real until it is delivered by hand. Pip had run the Sea's ten floors before breakfast and the Wood's before noon. He came over the ridge of the forty-first floor at dusk with his gold-winged heels smoking, and found the company at Cleaver's fire, and asked who was in charge, and was told nobody."], ["p", "He blew the horn."], ["p", "On every floor of the War, at once, the fighting stopped. The crimson lowered their spears. The azure lowered theirs. The ashen dead stood still in the mud, the way they had stood still in the real Field when the Arbiter first blew that horn at the Truce's swearing, in year 2, which none of them had lived to see. In the silence the Herald walked out between the lines with his white staff, as he had walked out ten thousand times, and for the first time somebody listened."], ["h", 2, "The truce on the Field"], ["p", "It took all night. The Field's rulers came to it one by one, because the worst of an hour does not give up easily."], ["p", "The **Last Marshal** of the forty-first floor, who had refused his king and led the azure and been taken from his tent before the battle, rode out on his horse of smoke and guarded both banners against the truce, because he had sworn to guard them and the war was all he had left to guard. [Sir Whiskers](heroes/sir-whiskers) went to meet him. They duelled in the mud by the light of the signal fires, and the Marshal was the better rider, and the lynx was the better liar, and in the end the Marshal's sword went through the lynx's guard, and Sir Whiskers died, and landed on his feet, and got up, one life fewer, and said, \"Do try again.\" The Marshal looked at him for a long time and then laughed and sheathed the sword."], ["p", "The **Iron Marshal**, the crimson's commander who had held the siege line through the Winter of Mud, came next, and then the **Siege Lord**, an Underforge engineer who had built the crimson's engines for pay and stayed to see them fire, and the **Ashen General** of the dead. The **Banner Knight**, the crimson's standard-bearer, had sworn the banner would never touch the ground, and had held it up for a hundred years; Cleaver gave him a bowl of truce-pot, which needs two hands, and he set the banner down to take it. The **Crow King**, a lord of Gloaming who had ridden to the crimson for a share of the sun, would not eat until the Countess [Vesper](heroes/vesper) sat down beside him and ate first. The **Black Herald** had been carrying King Orlan's last orders for a hundred years and had never been allowed to deliver them; Pip delivered them for him, by hand, to the Last Marshal, and the Marshal broke the seal and read them aloud. They were two words long. King Orlan, the night before the Ninth Field, had written to his enemy: *Come home.* The **Grey Captain**, who had deserted the azure on that night and regretted it on every floor, sat down at the fire and was not, any more, the only one who had walked away."], ["p", "And on the forty-eighth floor Cleaver found the drummer."], ["p", "He was a boy of thirteen, thin, beating the advance, with the dead getting up round him as he beat it. She walked up through them with a bowl in her hand, and he stopped drumming, because he knew her. She had caught him by the ear once. She sat him down on his drum and gave him the bowl, and he ate, and the dead lay down."], ["h", 2, "The first hour let go"], ["p", "Toward dawn, at Cleaver's fire, the Herald's truce was made. Both banners ate from one pot. Nobody fought within sight of it. The Last Marshal took off his helm and said his name, which the azure had not used in a hundred years, and his guard knelt to him in the mud. Sir Aldous of Nine, the Turncloak, who had fought for both banners one after the other and spent eight lives doing it, took off his helm too, at last, and his younger brother saw his face for the first time since the Field."], ["p", "Then something happened that had not happened since the Aumbry was made."], ["p", "The hour ended. The Ninth Field would never be fought, and the night before it, which the Tower had been keeping for a hundred years, came to a morning. The light changed on the Field. The signal fires burned down. The ashen soldiers, the dead of both banners, lay down in the mud and did not get up, and the living stood over them, and wept, and were tired, as people are at the end of a war. The whole floor shook, from the mud to the sky, the way a cupboard shakes when something inside it falls."], ["p", "Far below, in the Ring, the Arbiter felt it in his feet and looked up. Far above, in the Sun Court, Iselm put both hands on his dial."], ["p", "The Unmaker stood at the Field's edge and watched the war end. It was the one thing on the Dial it had truly unmade, the only war that could not end, because the Unmaker had walked across it; and it had ended anyway, because a cook and a courier and a Field's worth of tired people had decided that it would. Oblivion had wanted, for a hundred years, to know what losing was. It thought it knew now. It did not mind as much as it had expected."], ["p", "\"So that is how,\" it said, to nobody, and went on up."], ["h", 2, "The sparks again"], ["p", "The War's last floors were quiet after that. The armies were going home, wherever home was for them, now that the Tower could no longer keep them from it: down the stair, a long line of crimson and azure with the Herald at its head, looking for a gate. The company went on up through them."], ["p", "At the fiftieth floor, the War's crown and the top of the Climb, in a crater on the Field where the earth was still hot, burned **Ash and Ember** again: the Tower's keeping of the sun's two last sparks, at the instant they fell, as the Wood's crown had been. Above them, where the crater's sky should have been, there was a stair, going up into gold."], ["p", "They fought. They had to. And when they were down, they looked at [Anka](heroes/anka) a third time, and she did not ask."], ["p", "The brute of basalt on his knuckles spoke for the first time. His voice was a fire settling."], ["p", "\"The hand is opening,\" he said. \"We felt the war end. Come up when it opens all the way. Bring the rest.\""], ["p", "Anka asked him what the rest was."], ["p", "\"Everything that fell,\" said the flame in the burnt mantle. \"And the one who carried the lamp.\""], ["p", "Above them, up the stair, a great dial struck the hours of a day a hundred years gone, and it was nine weeks, near enough, since the company had come through the gate, and three days to midsummer."]]}, "story/11-above-the-fiftieth": {"title": "11. Above the Fiftieth", "sub": "Part five, the top. Three realms the Tower never showed, the choir still singing, the noon it took, and a window onto the first world it kept.", "blocks": [["h", 2, "The back rooms"], ["p", "Above the fiftieth floor the stair turned to gold and the floors lost their numbers. No climber had been here. These were the Tower's back rooms, where a keeper keeps its best pieces, and it had not shown them to anyone in a hundred years."], ["p", "The first was white. It was Frostfall in **the Long Hold**, the winter after the Fall that did not end until the next summer: the moors under snow to the horizon, the herds lying down in it, the shore villages of the frozen lake dark under a sky that could not decide what hour it was. In a croft above the lake a shepherd's family sat round a cold hearth, waiting for spring, as they had been waiting for a hundred years. Out on the ice, far off, a green and violet aurora hung over the lake at noon. There was no Winter Warden on the ice: the Tower had not been able to take her, because she had been fighting the cold with everything she had, and it had taken the people she was fighting for instead."], ["p", "Here [Tempo](heroes/tempo) found himself."], ["p", "He had seen himself on the War floors, a heartbeat ahead, going up. He had not understood. Now, on the snow above the lake, a white-haired man in a long dark coat with a clock-dial halo came toward him out of the falling snow, a heartbeat ahead of him in every step: the half of him the Tower had kept in the hour of noon, which had been climbing toward the Sun Court for a hundred years because it belonged to that hour and wanted to go back to it. The two of them stood face to face on the moor. Neither knew which was himself. Then the kept one said, \"Again,\" and the other said, \"And again,\" and they laughed, the same laugh a heartbeat apart, and walked on up together."], ["h", 2, "Aubade at dusk"], ["p", "The second was gold and grey. It was Aubade at dusk on the day of the Fall: the bell quarter, the Dawn Road running down from the morning cliffs, and at its foot the families of the hundred, waiting, with every bell in the city ringing because nobody knew what else to do. Far off up the road a girl with good legs was running toward them with a lamp in her hands, and she would never arrive, because this was the moment before she did."], ["p", "[Auriel](heroes/auriel) walked through the crowd. She was a hundred and looked twenty. She had been born at first light the next morning, and she knew her mother was not here, because her mother's waters had broken at dusk and she had gone home; but everyone else was, every family of the ninety-eight, holding each other at the foot of the road in their best clothes, looking up at the Tower for the singers to come down. They had been looking for a hundred years. The hundredth bell, which in her Aubade is always one short for the choir, was ringing here with all the others, because here nobody yet knew it should be short."], ["p", "She stood among them and looked up the road with them, the Dawn Paladin of the Noonsworn, in white and gold plate, and for a while she did not say anything at all. When she went on, she said to Cantor that she understood now what the Noonsworn were: a city that had stood at the foot of a road for a hundred years, waiting, and had called it faith."], ["h", 2, "Gloaming in the Long Dusk"], ["p", "The third was dark. It was Gloaming in **the Long Dusk**, the year without a day: the castle-city round the Night Temple under a sky that was always just past sunset, the vintners in their cellars and the sick in their beds and the light going out of the heights and never coming back. It was very beautiful. It was the age the Court of Dusk had called the Dial's true one."], ["p", "The Countess [Vesper](heroes/vesper) walked through her own city in the year that had made her. She had not been taken in it; she had spent that year feeding, and fighting the dark with blood, and the Tower takes only those who stand still. The ones who had stood still were here. She went into a house she knew, in the vintners' street, where a family she had known had nursed their old through the dark, and the old were in their beds, dying, as they had been dying for a hundred years, and could not finish. She stood by them a long while. Then she came out, and found [Nyx](heroes/nyx) waiting in her shadow, and said, \"This is what we were keeping,\" and Nyx said nothing, which was the right thing to say."], ["p", "These three realms are the Tower's deepest keepings, and the Story passes through them quickly, because each is a chapter for another telling: Frostfall in the Long Hold, Aubade at dusk, Gloaming in the Long Dusk."], ["h", 2, "The Sun Court"], ["p", "The stair came up out of the dark of Gloaming into white light, and the company stepped out onto the Sun Court at the Tower's top, above the clouds."], ["p", "It was as it had been on the day of the Fall. It was the day of the Fall. A court of white stone open to the sky, the great dial set into its floor, the lens at its edge through which the midsummer lamp had been lit; and across it, in rows, in their white and gold, ninety-eight singers of the choir of a hundred, standing still mid-note, their mouths open on the second line of the Hymn of Noon, in the hour of noon, where they had been standing for a hundred years. The master stood at their head with his breath drawn for the last verse. A gentle tenor stood one place to the right of an empty place."], ["p", "Auriel walked down the rows until she was in front of him. She had her father's face. He was thirty, and did not see her."], ["p", "Mum walked past them all to the master's right hand, to the empty place where she had stood, and stood in it, and Cantor took his place at the master's left. For a moment it was the choir of a hundred again, with two of its singers a hundred years older than the rest."], ["p", "Iselm was at his dial. He had come up his own stair and was waiting, as he had been waiting for every choir for four hundred years, and he received the company as if they were late. They were not. It was the night before the Hundredth Midsummer."], ["h", 2, "The Noon Seat"], ["p", "Above the Sun Court there was one more step: a round dais of white stone, open to the sky, where the sun had rested at noon. Climbers had called it the Noon Seat, and the Dial had said for a hundred years that it was empty, and that whoever reached it would decide what hung there. From below, through cloud, it had only ever looked like light."], ["p", "It was not empty. Over the Seat, so close you could have touched it, hung the noon the Tower had taken."], ["p", "It was not the sun. The sun's body had cracked and fallen; its heart was in the Hall of Noon a mile below. This was the rest of it, the part the Aumbry had been able to keep: its light, and its hour, and the gold of it, held still at the instant of noon on midsummer day a hundred years ago. It hung over the dais like a held breath, a gold so bright that it was quiet, and it did not warm anything, because warmth is a thing that moves, and nothing in it moved. Shiori wrote that it was the most beautiful thing she had ever seen, and the saddest, and then crossed out the second half, because it was not a measurement, and then wrote it in again."], ["p", "[Anka](heroes/anka) stood under it and burned, a little, without meaning to. She had come up the Tower to find the rest of the fire she came from. It was here, and it was not fire. It was what was left of the sun when you took away the burning."], ["h", 2, "The last window"], ["p", "Above the Seat, where the stair should have ended, a narrow stair went on up, and at its top was a window."], ["p", "[Pim and Pom](heroes/pim-and-pom) found it first, because nobody stopped them, and went through it, because nobody ever has. On the other side was the first world the Aumbry ever kept: Senmere, at its noon, a country of terraces under a red sun that did not move, white stone and blue glass and gardens and cats on the warm walls, and people on every terrace standing still and glad, as they had stood since before the Dial was counted."], ["p", "Not everyone was standing still. On a high terrace, under the red sun, children were playing. They had been at noon so long that they had forgotten every game but one, which they had invented themselves out of the only new thing that had happened in their sky in a thousand years: a silver thread that came down through it, now and then, from somewhere else, and that you could pull out, stitch by stitch, with small careful fingers, if you were patient. They had been unpicking the Sky-Stitcher's stitches in the Tear for twenty years. They did not know what the thread was for. They thought somebody was playing with them."], ["p", "Pim and Pom taught them hide and seek, properly, which is a game you can only play in a place where people move. It took the whole night. The children of Senmere were very bad at it and loved it. At the end of the night the girl at the high window of the pale tower in the middle of the city, who had been nine for a thousand years, leaned out and asked the twins what it was like, down there, where things changed. She had sent the same question down once before, on the tail of a kite that came up through the cloud. Pom told her. Pim said she should come and see."], ["h", 2, "The night before"], ["p", "On the Sun Court, among the singers who could not hear them, the company sat with Iselm through the night, and asked him questions, and he did not say no."], ["p", "The Tower would close its hand at noon. It would take the Ring and every champion in it, and the last of the Heart's light, and Frostfall and Aubade and Gloaming whole, not their hours but the realms themselves, and pull up its roots, and go out through the Tear into the dark, with everything it had kept, to find another ageing sun. It would leave behind a Dial with no sun, no hours and no Ring: the Ring Wick had seen through the hundredth door, at night, empty."], ["p", "It could be stopped only by its word. The Golemwright had been right: a made thing obeys the last word written on it. The Aumbry's word was cut at its root, in the small room under the floor where the shaft began, beneath the Hall of Noon, and it was Iselm's name, and so it was also in Iselm. The fox spirit could take it out of him, as she had taken her brother's name out of his mouth in year 40, so that for one breath the Tower would have no word at all. In that breath another word could be cut at the root, and the Tower would obey that one."], ["p", "Which word, they argued half the night. *End*, said the Unmaker, which was an ending and knew what it wanted. *Go*, said Sefu, who had come to hate the Tower. *Let go*, said Cantor, because that was what the last verse said, and Mum nodded. [Shiori](heroes/shiori), the last of the Hourkeepers' great calligraphers, whose writing comes true, said none of those. The Hourkeepers have always known what to do with a thing that cannot be kept: you write it down, and let it go, and keep the record."], ["p", "\"*Remember*,\" she said."], ["p", "Iselm did not answer that one either way. After a long time he said, \"That would have been a better word,\" and looked out at the terraces of Senmere through the window above the Seat, and at the girl at her window, and did not say anything else."], ["p", "Shiori wrote the word in the script of Senmere, which he had taught her on the fortieth floor, on a single sheet of the Hourkeepers' paper, with her tall brush, in one stroke, as her way requires. Then they needed someone to carry it a mile straight down, to the room under the Heart, between the first line of the last verse and the last, and the last verse is not long."], ["p", "[Pip](heroes/pip) said that a letter is not delivered until it is delivered by hand, and held out his hand."], ["p", "Toward dawn, up the outside of the Tower, through the cloud, came a paper dragon on a long line, with a young woman of the Spires steering it from its back and a young man beside her with a very long jade scarf, both of them shouting. The Kite Master had answered the message on her kite's tail at last. [Zephyr](heroes/zephyr) had come to stand on the Noon Seat for one heartbeat, and then jump. He looked at Pip, and at the page in Pip's hand, and at the mile of air below the Sun Court's edge, and grinned."], ["p", "\"Race you down,\" he said."]]}, "story/12-the-last-verse": {"title": "12. The Last Verse", "sub": "Part five, noon on the Hundredth Midsummer. At the top, at the foot and under the Heart, at once.", "blocks": [["h", 2, "Morning at the Ring"], ["p", "At dawn on the Hundredth Midsummer the Ring was full. The Truce had asked the Ringside to go home. Forty thousand people had come anyway, because it was the Opening of the Ring and the hundredth anniversary of the Fall, and because, if the Tower was going to take the Ring at noon, they had decided they would rather be in it."], ["p", "Its officers held it."], ["p", "The Saltwarden walked the Ring's edge before first light with her pouch, as she did every dawn, and then kept walking: out of the stands, across the crater's floor, round the Ring and the stands and the Truce Table and the Tally-house and the couriers' loft, a line of salt four miles round. She had always wanted to lay a line round the Tower to see which side it was on. The line went round the Ring, and the Tower stood outside it."], ["p", "The Chainwarden brought up every chain of the Gaol of Oaths, forged in the Ember Caldera, her grandmother's chains, and ran them from the Gaol's great anchor under the harbour road all the way to the Tower's foot, and round it, three times, and locked them. \"You can run to the end of the chain,\" she told it. \"Then you come back.\""], ["p", "Under the stands, [Wick](heroes/wick) walked the gallery of the Hundred Doors with his lamp. He was a hundred and fifty years old. All night the doors had been warm, and since dawn they had been pushing, all of them at once, from the other side, like doors in a house where someone is opening every window. He had sworn to keep them. He put his back against the hundredth, and held it, and the lamp he had kept lit in front of it for ninety-six years burned steady."], ["p", "[Referee Whistler](heroes/referee-whistler) stood in the Arbiter's box with no horn, because a courier had it, and a sigil tablet in his hand cut with the fifth clause. [Mr. Ledger](heroes/mr-ledger) stood beside him with the Truce's bill. [Madame Cleaver](heroes/madame-cleaver), come down from the War floors with her cauldron, fed the stands. [Grandma Ironknit](heroes/grandma-ironknit) sat in the front row with her needles and her grey cat, knitting, and her face had the look of a woman who has found a dropped stitch at last."], ["p", "And in the straw-rope circle at the Ring's heart, where the Tower's shadow had lain since the Fall, [Big Yoko](heroes/big-yoko) stood with his feet in it, as he had stood for a hundred years, and threw a handful of salt, and waited."], ["h", 2, "Noonfall"], ["p", "A mile beneath them, in the hall between the court and the pit, two champions of the Ring went down to fight the Raid's three crowns with the Truce's leave: the Sunbearer, with the Ember of Aubade in its brass shrine on his back, and the Obsidian Herald of the Underforge, with her blade of black glass and a word in her mouth she had never said."], ["p", "The Sun Warden's sentinel, the stone and gold likeness the Tower had made of its keeper to guard the court, met them at the first gate. They broke it. Ash and Ember came up out of the Cinder Pit at the second, two shapes of fire, the sun's last sparks themselves, bound to guard the Heart. They fought, because they were bound; and when they were down they did not go out. They looked at the brass shrine on the Sunbearer's back, and at the light inside it, and stood aside."], ["p", "\"The one who carried the lamp,\" said Ember."], ["p", "Under the hall, in the Hall of Noon, the Heart was waiting. For a hundred years the Tower had drunk its light every morning as it rose; it was a wounded and furious thing, white and huge, and it had fought every pair of champions the Truce had sent down, and it fought these two. It was very nearly noon. Above them the whole Tower had begun to hum."], ["p", "The Obsidian Herald did what the Caldera's fire had asked her to do when she was fifteen. She walked into the Heart's light, close enough to blister, and said the word it had given her, aloud, for the first time. It was the sun's own name, older than the Dial, older than the Hymn: the name the sun had before anyone called it father or noon. The Heart heard its name and was, for a moment, not a wound but a sun, and stopped."], ["p", "The Sunbearer walked into the stillness. He had sworn never to set the Ember down except to fight. The Noonsworn had always said it went up, to the Noon Seat, to be hung in the sky; the Caldera's fire had always said, to the Obsidian Herald, that it was waiting for him down here. He unbuckled the brass shrine from his back, the first time it had been off his back in ten years, and opened it, and set the Ember of Aubade, the last light of the old sun anyone could hold, into the Heart, as you set a seed in the ground. Then he sat down on the black glass floor beside it, because it was very heavy, and he was very tired."], ["p", "Beneath the Heart, the rock that no drill had broken broke."], ["h", 2, "The root"], ["p", "The Underforge had been digging toward that rock for nine weeks, under the Heart, in the dark, with the Truce's leave and the Hall of Marks pretending not to know. The Hallbreaker, the sapper, had at last finished the charge she had never finished on purpose: the one that does not need to know where the wall is. [Mabel](heroes/mabel) had dug the gallery to it with her claws, following the high note in her lantern crystal. [Bram](heroes/bram) had come because he had always wanted to touch the Tower's stone and find out who had cut it, and he had brought Gerald. [Ada](heroes/ada) had built the lift. The Golemwright waited at the face with a chisel and a mallet and the script of Senmere in his head, and Brannoc behind him with a light, because the coal in his chest was the only fire down there."], ["p", "The Hallbreaker lit the fuse. The charge burned fuchsia and found the wall, wherever it was, and the rock under the Heart opened like a book."], ["p", "Behind it was a small room of grey stone with no seam in it, where the shaft began. Roots as thick as towers went out from it in every direction, under everything. On its wall, cut deep, in a script nobody on the Dial but two people could read, was one word."], ["p", "Bram put his hand on it. Gerald, under his other arm, said nothing, because Gerald never says anything; but Bram heard, as he sometimes hears stone, the mason who had cut it, a careful man, grieving. \"Oh,\" said Bram. \"Oh, it was him.\""], ["p", "The Golemwright raised his chisel and waited. He could not cut a new word over the old one. A golem obeys the last word written on it, but only while no other word is there; and this one was the strongest word he had ever seen."], ["h", 2, "Noon"], ["p", "At the top of the Tower, at noon, the Aumbry closed its hand."], ["p", "It did it the way it had done it a hundred years before, the way it had done it on Senmere: all at once, and gently, as a hand closes on a moth. The Sun Court shook. The ninety-eight singers in their rows trembled like candle flames. Over the Noon Seat the kept noon blazed. Above it the Tear opened, and opened, a black mouth with a burning pink edge that filled half the sky, and the dark between the stars looked down through it at the Dial. Across the Dial the ground heaved as the roots began to pull: the Walking Wood broke into a run and came over the crater's rim in a green wave and stopped, roots in the grey stone, at the edge of the Saltwarden's salt; the sea drew back from the Drowned Coast; the snow lifted off the moors of Frostfall; the bells of Aubade began to ring by themselves. The Chainwarden's chains drew tight round the Tower's foot and sang. The Hundred Doors heaved against Wick's back. The Tower's shadow, which had lain on the Ring for a hundred years, pulled at Big Yoko's feet like a tide, and he set them, and did not move."], ["p", "And at the master's right hand, in the place where she had stood a hundred years before, Mum sang."], ["h", 2, "The last verse"], ["p", "It was the first sound she had made since the Fall. Not a word, not a note, not a laugh, not a cry, for a hundred years; and then the first line of the last verse, in the old tongue of Senmere, in the purest voice in the history of Aubade, a hundred years out of practice and a hair behind the beat."], ["p", "[Cantor](heroes/cantor), at the master's left, came in on the beat, under her, as if he had always known it, because he had been listening to her for a hundred years and knew where she would go."], ["p", "The master heard it."], ["p", "He had been standing with his breath drawn for the last verse for a hundred years, and he let it out, and sang. The verse is a verse of evening and of letting go, and a kept hour cannot hold someone who is singing it. Down the rows of the choir it went like a wind through grass: the ninety-eight woke into the verse, one after another, the steadiest tenor in the hundred among them, on the beat, and Auriel, standing in front of him, heard her father's voice for the first time in her life, and he saw her, and did not know her, and knew her."], ["p", "Far off, faint, from the east, the bells of Aubade answered: a hundred bells, ringing on the beat, and for the first time in a hundred years none of them short. The Bellwright had stayed in the bell tower to ring the hundredth herself."], ["h", 2, "The word taken"], ["p", "Iselm stood at his dial with his hands flat on it, as he had been kneeling on the day of the Fall, and the word that was his name strained in him like a rope. He could feel the Aumbry fighting the verse. It had been made to keep; the verse was the only thing that had ever made it let go, and it was being sung, by the voice it had waited ten years at an open gate to catch, and it was angry, in the patient, enormous way of a made thing that does not know what anger is."], ["p", "He did the one thing the word allowed him. He said his own name, aloud, as you say a name to someone who has asked for it."], ["p", "\"Iselm.\""], ["p", "The fox spirit took it out of the air as he said it, the way she takes every spell said aloud near her. For a moment she held in her two hands the word that had moved the Aumbry across the dark between the stars, and it was the heaviest thing she had ever stolen, and she could not hold it long. She threw it to her brother."], ["p", "[The Great Zambini](heroes/the-great-zambini) caught it in his hat."], ["p", "He had played every court on the Dial and been thirty-nine for sixty years and was fading, slowly, in the empty hours between performances. He had always wanted an audience at the Noon Seat: the whole Dial, watching, at once. He had it. Across the whole Dial, at noon on the Hundredth Midsummer, everyone was looking up at the Tower, as Senmere had once looked up at its sun, because the Tear had opened over it and the sky was falling in; and what the whole Dial saw, at the very top, small and clear against the gold, was a figure in a violet cape and a tall hat with a gold star, who bowed, and showed them his hat, empty, and put it on his head."], ["p", "The Aumbry had no word."], ["h", 2, "The fall"], ["p", "For one breath, everything hung. The Tower did not keep and did not let go. The floors did not move. The Tear stopped widening. The ninety-eight singers sang the last verse and the kept noon over the Seat neither held nor fell, and a mile below, in the room under the Heart, the old word on the wall went pale and empty, like a page, and the Golemwright raised his chisel."], ["p", "He had nothing to cut."], ["p", "At the Sun Court's edge [Pip](heroes/pip) the courier stood with Shiori's page in one hand and the Arbiter's horn in the other, and the Truce's challenge delivered (he had given it to Iselm at dawn, by hand, and Iselm had read it, and signed it received, which was the only answer the word allowed). [Zephyr](heroes/zephyr) stood on the Noon Seat beside him, as he had always wanted, for one heartbeat."], ["p", "Then they jumped."], ["p", "It is a mile from the Sun Court to the Ring, straight down the Tower's grey face, and Zephyr's wind took them for most of it, a gust for every dash, the two fastest people on the Dial racing each other down the outside of the Tower through the cloud with the page between them, and the Kite Master's paper dragon diving after them like a falling kite. [Pim](heroes/pim-and-pom) slid down the outside behind them on a fold of the dragon's tail, shrieking with joy, as he had always wanted to; Pom stayed at the top and laughed. At the last moment Zephyr's wind ran out and the dragon caught them both and dropped them on the Ring's sand in front of Big Yoko, who caught Pip in one hand, and set him on his feet, and said, \"Run.\""], ["p", "Pip ran. Across the Ring, down the west tunnel, through the hall of Noonfall past Ash and Ember, down the stair into the Hall of Noon past a knight sitting on the floor beside the Heart, down the Underforge's new shaft, into the room under the Tower, where a golem-maker stood with a chisel raised in front of a blank wall. He put Shiori's page into the Golemwright's hand."], ["p", "\"Sign here,\" said Pip, out of breath. \"And here. And there, where it's on fire.\""], ["p", "The Golemwright looked at the word in Shiori's one stroke, in the script of Senmere, and cut it into the stone where the old word had been, in her hand, as the word on every golem's brow is in her hand."], ["p", "*Remember.*"], ["p", "At the top, Mum sang the last line of the last verse, and the Aumbry obeyed the last word written on it."], ["h", 2, "The hand opens"], ["p", "It did not let go of everything at once. A keeper that remembers lets go the way an old man opens a box of letters: carefully, one at a time, reading each."], ["p", "It let go of the noon. Over the Noon Seat the held gold moved, for the first time in a hundred years, like a breath let out. Up through the Tower from the Heart, a mile below, came the Heart's light, undrunk for the first time since the Fall, all of it, the way heat comes up a flue, with the Ember of Aubade burning in the middle of it like a seed in a fruit; and up the same flue, out of the hall of Noonfall, came Ash and Ember, the sun's last sparks, roaring. \"Everything that fell,\" they had said. \"Bring the rest.\" [Anka](heroes/anka) stood on the Noon Seat under the moving gold and asked them one last time whether an Ember could choose to go home."], ["p", "\"Yes,\" they said. \"Now.\""], ["p", "She stepped into the noon. Ash and Ember met her in it, and the Heart's light rose into it, and the Ember of Aubade, and for a long moment the Noon Seat was too bright to look at. [Silvane](heroes/silvane), who had come up the last stair with her sister, opened the hand mirror in which she had caught the sun's last light when she was twelve, and let it out: the sun's last look, which had been looking at her for a hundred years, went back to where it belonged. [Opaline](heroes/opaline) held up a crystal from the bottom of the Prism Stair with a colour in it that has no name, the colour the sun had lost, and gave it back."], ["p", "When the light could be looked at again, there was a sun over the Tower."], ["p", "It was small, and young, and gold, and it was moving. It rose off the Noon Seat as the Ember of Aubade had once come down the Dawn Road, steadily, and stood over the Tower at noon, and the Dial, looking up, felt it on their faces, warm, which nobody under sixty had ever felt. Sefu stood under it on the edge of the Seat, where a king of the Sands had always said a king of the Sands would sit, and did not sit. He took the cursed crown off his head, and set it on the white stone, on no head at all. The Uncrowned, beside him, laughed for the first time in a hundred years. \"It's yours now,\" she said. \"It never stays.\" It stayed where he had put it. Vesper stood in Nyx's shadow at the Seat's foot, out of the noon, and watched the sun rise that would burn her, and did nothing to stop it."], ["h", 2, "Senmere's evening"], ["p", "It let go of Senmere last."], ["p", "Through the window above the Seat the red sun of the first world it had ever kept began to move, after a thousand years at noon, and the terraces of Senmere had their afternoon at last: long, and gold, and then red. The people on the terraces stopped being glad and still, and were glad and moving, and then, as the light went down, tired, and then quiet, as people are at the end of a long day. Linnet at her window finished her question."], ["p", "Iselm went up the last stair. He was very old now, older than anything on the Dial, and with his name gone he was nothing but a careful man who had made something out of grief. He looked back once at the company on the Sun Court, and at the new sun over the Tower, and at Mum, who had sung his verse. Then he went through the window into Senmere's evening, and up through the terraces to the pale tower in the middle of the city, and sat down on the edge of his daughter's bed as the red sun went down, and answered her."], ["p", "The Unmaker followed him. It was the evening of Senmere, and Senmere was having its evening now; it had somewhere to be at last. [Oblivion](heroes/oblivion) stopped at the window and looked back at Wick's Dial, the doors and the hours it had fought for a hundred years, and said, \"There was something here,\" and then, because it had learned the Dial's tongue well, \"Thank you.\" Then it went through into the dusk of its own world, and was the dusk, and ended, as everything ends."], ["p", "Above the Tower the Tear began to close. The Sky-Stitcher stood at the Seat's edge, nearer to it than anyone had ever been, and sewed: two edges of the sky drawn together with a golden thread, stitch by stitch. On the other side, small careful fingers held the edges for her this time. The children of Senmere had learned one more game. The last stitch went in at dusk, Senmere's dusk, and the dark between the stars was gone, and the sky over the Tower was only sky."], ["p", "Under the Ring, the Hundred Doors stopped pushing. Wick felt the largest door on the Dial close behind him, gently, and the lamp in front of the hundredth went out on its own, for the first time in ninety-six years, because it was no longer needed."], ["h", 2, "The shadow moves"], ["p", "In the Ring, in the straw-rope circle, with the new sun over the Tower and forty thousand people on their feet, Big Yoko looked down at the shadow across his feet. It was not lying still any more. It was trembling, like a dog that wants to go."], ["p", "He had stood in the circle since the Fall. He had always believed that if he stepped out, the shadow would move. He had stood there through a war and a hundred years of the Truce, and lost once, and told nobody, and been very patient. He would have liked, he had told the company, for whoever climbed the Tower to come back down and step out of the ring when it was time."], ["p", "He stepped out."], ["p", "The Tower's shadow moved. For the first time in a hundred years it left the Ring and went sweeping out across the crater, round the Dial, like the hand of a clock, telling every realm its hour; and every realm, for the first time in a hundred years, agreed what time it was."]]}, "story/13-after": {"title": "13. After", "sub": "Part five, the first days under the new sun. What came out of the Tower, what went across, and what each of them got.", "blocks": [["h", 2, "The first afternoon"], ["p", "The new sun did what the old one had done before the Fall, and what nobody under a hundred remembered a sun doing. It walked. It went down the sky from the Tower in the afternoon, gold and then red, and the Tower's shadow walked round the Dial ahead of it, and at the hour of dusk, the true hour, the sun set in the Outer Sea in the west and the sky over Gloaming went dark at the right time. Then it was night, a whole night, with stars, and no Tear among them. In the morning the sun came up out of the sea in the east, over the morning cliffs of Aubade, and the bells rang it up, and it rose."], ["p", "The Hourkeepers' Slip-sheet for that month was one line long. *The hours run true.*"], ["h", 2, "The market comes out"], ["p", "The Tower let go the way an old man opens a box of letters, one at a time, reading each, and the first thing it opened was the first thing it had taken after the sun."], ["p", "On the morning after the Hundredth Midsummer the gate of the Tower opened from inside, and out of it, onto the grey stone of the crater, walked the midsummer market of year 0: a mile of stalls and a thousand families, glass from the Sands and wool from Frostfall and Gloaming wine and iron from the hill people who were not yet grey, all of them blinking in the sunlight and wondering where the grass had gone. It was still, for them, a few minutes after noon on midsummer day. Among them, with a paving stone in his arms, came a Kilnborn stonecutter who was not yet grey, looking for his youngest son, who was meant to be sweeping the wrestling circle."], ["p", "The Arbiter of the Ring came down out of his box. He was a hundred and fourteen and looked thirty-four, in a black helm with a red crest and a black and ivory tabard, and his skin had gone grey as ash in his twenties as Kilnborn skin does, even far from the forge. His father did not know him. Then he did. [Referee Whistler](heroes/referee-whistler) took off his helm in front of forty thousand people, which he had never done, and his father put down the paving stone and looked at his son's face, a grown man's face, grey, a hundred years late, and said the only thing a stonecutter could think of to say, which was that the boy's line was still crooked."], ["p", "Whistler laughed. The Ring had not heard him laugh in a hundred years."], ["p", "The Countess of Gloaming's steward's daughter walked out of the market with a basket of Coast fish, and was met at the crater's rim by a very old man from Lantern-under-Hill who had been told, at dawn, by a messenger of the Court, to come. Grandma Ironknit, in the front row with her needles, picked up two stitches she had dropped a hundred years before, and knitted them back into the cloth, and put a third, which had never ended, back where it belonged, with a little knot."], ["p", "[Mr. Ledger](heroes/mr-ledger) walked through the market with his red book, looking at every stall, because a market of the old coinage was an accountant's dream, and on a money-changer's tray, among Aubade bells and Sands crooks and Gloaming moons, he found a single bronze obol of the Truce, stamped with the Ring and the Tower's shadow, struck forty years after his death, which a Hundred Door had let fall into the market long ago and which the money-changer had been puzzling over, in his hour, for a hundred years. It was the coin missing from his sum. He bought it back, at a fair rate."], ["h", 2, "The choir comes down"], ["p", "At noon the ninety-eight came down the Tower's stair, singing, as the choir had come down every evening for four hundred years, with the light still on their faces."], ["p", "They came down the Dawn Road to Aubade in the afternoon. At the foot of the road, where the families of the hundred had waited until the last light a hundred years before, there was a crowd waiting again, because the whole city had come out. In it was an old woman of a hundred and twenty-five who looked forty-five, Noon-touched, with her daughter beside her in white and gold plate. The steadiest tenor in the hundred came down the road and saw his wife, who had been nine months gone when he left her that morning, and his daughter, who was a hundred years old and looked twenty, and he stopped on the road with the choir going on round him, and [Auriel](heroes/auriel) ran up it, as she had never run up anything."], ["p", "The bells rang a hundred, that night, and none of them was for anyone missing."], ["p", "The master of the choir went to [Mum](heroes/mum) when they reached the city and asked her, as masters do, whether she would teach the last verse to someone else, now, in case anything happened to her. She said yes. She said it aloud. It was the first word she had spoken in a hundred years, and [Cantor](heroes/cantor), who was standing next to her because he had decided he would always be standing next to her, said that it was the most beautiful note he had ever heard, and she told him he was a hair behind, and he was."], ["h", 2, "The dead go across"], ["p", "Not everyone the Tower had kept could walk out. Many of the people of its floors had been dead when it took them: the drowned dockers on the quays of Sallowmouth, the ashen soldiers of the Banners, the old of Gloaming dying in the Long Dusk who could not finish. A keeper that remembers does not keep the dead from going. They went."], ["p", "On the Climb's floors the rivers of pale light that the ferryman of the dead lays wherever he poles his boat had run uphill for ten years, because the dead of the floors could not cross. Now they ran down. The ferryman of the dead poled his boat up through the Tower floor by floor, and took them across, all of them: the drowned of the Sea, the dead of the Wood's first year, the buried caravans of the Sands, the miners of the far halls, the ashen dead of both banners. On the Deep's floors, in the black water, he found a girl's body that had been in the Tower for four hundred years, since the night he drowned carrying it across a flood, and finished her crossing. It was the crossing his Reason had kept him for. He did not lie down. He found, to his surprise, that he had become fond of the work."], ["p", "[Mortis](heroes/mortis) helped him, which nobody had ever seen and the ferryman of the dead did not mention. The Restless of the Dial lay down that summer in numbers the Ossuary Lord had never recorded, as the light grew and the curve on his desk turned over and fell. He sat at his desk one night and wrote the sentence he had died finishing, three hundred years before: the answer to why the dead get up. *The dead get up because something will not let them end.* He wrote all of it but the last word, and looked at it a long time, and put the pen down, and went out to see about the War's dead. He is keeping the last word for a night when he is tired."], ["p", "[Marin](heroes/marin) danced the tide out of the Drowned Harbour on the first floor, as she had danced it out of the temple, and the drowned of Sallowmouth went down the stair with her and out to the real harbour and into the sea, home; and a boy with a coral flower behind his ear looked back at her once. The Bell-Warden stopped ringing. [Gus](heroes/gus) swept the temple floor behind them, and left it wet."], ["h", 2, "What came out of the floors"], ["p", "The living came out of the gate for weeks."], ["p", "From the Sea came the Bell-Warden and the Net-Father and Tam the net-mender, a third of Sallowmouth, to a coast where their grandchildren were old; and the Bellwright's old cracked bell, the Bell Below, which she carried home to Aubade and hung beside the new one. From the Wood came the Moss-Abbess, both of her, the young one walking out arm in arm with the old one, and the Moot speaker of year 90, and the Elder Root: the oak Fenna had planted at twelve walked out of the Tower's gate on its own roots and stood in the crater, where the Walking Wood had stopped at the edge of the Saltwarden's salt, and put its roots down, and the Wood round it stopped walking and stood still, for the first time since the Fall, round the Ring, as a forest. The cut on [Hank](heroes/hank)'s hand healed in a day. [Fenna](heroes/fenna) planted a flower at the oak's foot."], ["p", "From the Sands came the caravan towns of the Storm Canyon and a star-reader who climbed the first hill she found and saw, for the first time in her life, the stars. From the Deep came the miners of the far halls, and the Echo-Hermit, who said the rock had gone quiet. From the War came the living of both banners: the Last Marshal, who went to the Barrow of Harrow and stood over his king's grave and read him back his own orders; the Herald; the White Sister, home to Lowlight; the Camp-Mother, who went to work at the Truce Table with Cleaver; Sir Aldous of Nine, on his ninth life, who went back to the Keep of Nine with his brother to live it out slowly; and a drummer boy of thirteen whom [Madame Cleaver](heroes/madame-cleaver) took home and fed, and kept feeding, and in time adopted. The Black Herald had delivered his orders, and lay down."], ["p", "Of the climbers who had gone up the Tower between year 90 and year 99 and never come down, most came down."], ["h", 2, "The Noon-touched"], ["p", "The Hourkeepers had feared, for a hundred years, that if the noon were ever let go, everyone held a little in it would age all at once. It was let go. Nobody aged at all."], ["p", "What the Tower had held of the Noon-touched was not owed. It had not been kept from them; it had only been kept. When the door opened, nothing came due. They began, that summer, to age as people always had: one year in one. Auriel will look twenty-one next summer, and twenty-two the summer after, and she has said it is the best news of her life. Cantor and Mum are growing old together, at the ordinary speed, which they both consider very fast. The Arbiter will be thirty-five by the Opening."], ["p", "[Tempo](heroes/tempo) did not become one. The two of him had walked into the Sun Court a heartbeat apart, and a heartbeat apart they stayed: the Tower was a clock again, and a clock at the Dial's centre needs a keeper at its dial now that its maker has gone. One Tempo stayed at the top, at Iselm's great dial, winding it. The other went home to Frostfall, to the great clock in the observatory. They keep time a heartbeat apart, and the Dial's hours run true between them, and neither has ever asked which one is himself."], ["h", 2, "The debts"], ["p", "[Referee Whistler](heroes/referee-whistler) gave his ruling on the steps of the Tally-house, on the first evening, under the new sunset: the Tower had been bound by the fifth clause of the Truce, like any party that sat in the Truce's shadow; it had taken, and it was paying. The Truce held. The Ring would open next midsummer as it always had. The gate would open, as the fourth clause said, for any champion of the Ring, and it would be the Truce's to keep."], ["p", "[Mr. Ledger](heroes/mr-ledger) wrote the Tower's account into the Tally that night, every taking and every return, in the plainest hand he had: the longest page in the book. With the coin from the money-changer's tray the Tally balanced, for the first time in a hundred years, to the last obol, which should have finished his Reason and laid him down. It did not, because he has always kept one debt open on purpose, his own, to Grandma Ironknit, for the scarf. He still wears it under the collar."], ["p", "The Countess [Vesper](heroes/vesper) paid the principal on the Court of Dusk's note, the grain of the Long Dusk, a hundred years late, in full, in person, and took no receipt. Her Court lives by night, as Gloaming did before the Fall, and stays indoors at noon, as Gloaming did then too. It has found a new purpose: Gloaming's Long Dusk is still in the Tower, among the keepings the Tower has not yet opened, and when it opens, somebody must walk its dying out of it, and the Court of Dusk has said it will."], ["p", "The Uncrowned paid Vitriol for the curse at last, with one obol, the price never having been set, and the embalmer, who could have lifted it any night for a hundred years, lifted it that night. The crown of the Sands stayed where Sefu had put it, on the Noon Seat, on no head, and is there still. [Sefu](heroes/sefu) stopped drinking his draught. He rules the Sands with his sister now, which they both dislike, and the Throne's judge weighs every hard question for them, and finds most of them heavy with grief."], ["h", 2, "The others"], ["p", "[The Great Zambini](heroes/the-great-zambini) has stopped fading. The whole Dial watched him at noon on the Hundredth Midsummer, and the Dial does not forget a man who made a sky stop falling with a hat; he is solid to the fingertips, and thirty-nine, and insufferable. [Big Yoko](heroes/big-yoko) walked out of the Ring for the first time in a hundred years and went home to the shrine cliffs below Aubade, and came back for the Opening, and stood in the circle again, because he liked it. [Brannoc](heroes/brannoc)'s coal burns again, from the Heart's fire freed in the Caldera; he kept the promise he and Cleaver had made in the Winter of Mud, and the inn at the Waymeet is open. [Professor Puff](heroes/professor-puff) took the basket up the outside of the Tower at last and mapped every floor's sky, and [Ada](heroes/ada) went down to the room under the Heart to look at the gears and came back saying there were none, only roots, and a word. Bo's sheep, on the high pastures, at noon on midsummer, did not turn toward the Tower. They went on eating. [Lyra](heroes/lyra) charted the stars over the closed Tear on the first night, and found them on no chart, and found among them, larger than before, her red star."], ["p", "[Anka](heroes/anka) did not come back. She had asked whether an Ember could choose to go home, and been told yes, and she is the sun's warmth now, which everyone on the Dial can feel on their face at noon. But the Heartweigher still has her feather, and on the morning of the next midsummer the fishermen off the Drowned Coast saw a spark fall out of the new sun into the sea, and did not go out. Mortis has been told. He says he will believe it when he has buried it."]]}, "story/14-the-floors-above": {"title": "14. The Floors Above", "sub": "Epilogue. A Tower that remembers, the keepings it has not yet opened, what came through the Tear before it closed, and a red star.", "blocks": [["h", 2, "The Tower asleep"], ["p", "On the first night of the new sun, the moth priestess flew up the outside of the Tower at dusk, as she had flown over the Walking Wood for a hundred years to lay it down to rest, and shook the dust of her wings over its top. She had dreamed, awake, since the Fall, that the Tower had not slept in all that time either, and was dreaming. She was right. It slept. It is the only thing on the Dial that the moth priestess has ever laid to sleep and heard, afterwards, say thank you."], ["p", "It still stands at the Dial's centre, a shaft of pale grey stone with no seam in it, its top in the cloud, and its shadow walks round the Dial every day and tells every realm its hour. It is the Dial's gnomon again, and its clock, with a clockmaker at its dial. It does not keep. It remembers."], ["p", "Every floor it ever filled is still there, and the gate still opens for a champion of the Ring. Climb it, and you walk the Drowned Harbour on the morning the water came, the Walking Wood in its first year, the Storm Canyon in the storm, the far halls in the dark, the Field on the last night of the war: the Tower's memory of each hour, exact to the gull and the grain of sand. The people are gone out of them. What is left is the hour itself, and the worst of it, which the Tower remembers as clearly as the rest: the Drowned Admiral still rides his wreck, the bark hounds still run, the dead still get up on the Field when the drummer's memory beats. Champions climb it, as they always did, for the right to climb and for what it teaches; the Hourkeepers call it the best record of the Dial ever made, and send their young up it to learn history. It is still the Climb. It is only that now, when you fall on a floor, nothing keeps you there."], ["h", 2, "The keepings not yet opened"], ["p", "The Tower is letting go the way an old man opens a box of letters, one at a time, reading each, and it has not finished."], ["p", "Behind the Climb's fifty floors, in its back rooms, are the three realms it never showed. They have not been let go yet, because an hour let go needs someone to walk its people out of it, as Gus walked the frightened out of the sea temple, and these three are the largest."], ["ul", ["**Frostfall in the Long Hold.** The moors under snow, the herds lying down, the shore villages waiting in the dark for a spring that would not come. The Winter Warden [Isolde](heroes/isolde) has said she will go in, with her sister the aurora dancer, who will dance the aurora over the lake at noon, as she danced it on the day of the Fall, and this time, she thinks, the sun will answer. The cold spoke to Isolde once, in the Long Hold, and told her that if the Tower's hour were set going it would have to let go of everything it keeps, the library, the lake, and her. It is going. She does not know yet what she will be when it has.", "**Aubade at dusk on the day of the Fall.** When the choir came down the Dawn Road, there were families missing from the crowd at its foot: the ones the Tower took at dusk, standing still in their grief, waiting for the singers. They are still waiting, in the Tower's back room, at the foot of a road with every bell ringing, for a hundred singers who have already come home. The Noonsworn mean to go in and bring them down the road to meet them. It will be the hardest homecoming in the history of the city, and the bells are ready.", "**Gloaming in the Long Dusk.** The year without a day, the old dying in their beds and unable to finish. The Court of Dusk has sworn to walk its dying out of it, and the ferryman of the dead to take them across, and [Mortis](heroes/mortis), who has never let a dead thing lie down if it could work, has asked, to everyone's surprise, to go too."]], ["p", "Deeper still, in rooms the Tower took long before the Fall, are older keepings: the **revel of the Fox court** behind the thirty-first door, which [The Great Zambini](heroes/the-great-zambini) and the fox spirit mean to open from the inside, together, without stealing anyone's name; the **rooms of the Spires** that nobody built, from whose salt [Professor Puff](heroes/professor-puff) distils his rose gas without knowing what it is; and the single lives the Tower took, now and then, for a thousand years, through its roots and its doors. Nobody knows yet how many there are. [Shiori](heroes/shiori) has begun the list. It is long."], ["h", 2, "What came through the Tear"], ["p", "The Tear did not close at once. For the length of the last verse it hung wide over the Tower, the widest it had ever been, onto the dark between the stars; and the Aumbry was not the only thing that travels that dark."], ["p", "Three shapes came down its last edge as the Sky-Stitcher drew it shut, and went into the ground at the Tower's foot, under Noonfall, among the roots. The Hourkeepers have not named them. The Truce has heard them, at night, under the hall where the Raid was fought, and the Heart of Noon, which burns free in the Hall of Noon now and is no longer anyone's to guard, flares when they move. And the **Dawnless**, the shadow of the Cast that hated the dawn and carried sleepers to the Tower for a hundred years, fled down the roots when the hand opened, with [Nyx](heroes/nyx) and the Huntmaster a step behind it. It is somewhere under the Dial, in the dark, where the Tower's roots still run. They are still hunting it, together. The Huntmaster says every trail ends somewhere. Nyx has started, very occasionally, to answer him."], ["p", "The Truce has reopened Noonfall to the Ring's pairs. The Raid is not over."], ["h", 2, "The red star"], ["p", "On the night before the Hundredth Midsummer, on the Sun Court, among all the questions the company asked Iselm, there was one they did not think to ask, and the twins asked it, the next morning, of the girl at the window, because it was the kind of question a child asks. Whether Senmere had made only one."], ["p", "The girl at the window had looked at them with her father's careful face and said nothing at all, which, in that family, is not no."], ["p", "[Lyra](heroes/lyra)'s red star, the one she found at ten low over the Tear and named for herself, is larger every month. It is not a star. It moves against the others, slowly, toward the Dial, the way the Aumbry once moved through the dark toward an ageing sun. But the Dial's sun is not ageing now. It is the youngest sun anyone has ever seen, a hundred days old, small and gold, with an Ember in it who once walked out of the sea; and whatever is coming toward it has come a very long way, and is perhaps not coming to take."], ["p", "Or perhaps it is. The Dial knows, now, what to do with a thing that keeps. It knows the last verse, which a soprano of Aubade is teaching, aloud, to a child of the bell quarter, in case anything happens to her. It knows a word, cut in the script of a world that has had its evening, in the root of the Tower, in a calligrapher's one stroke. It knows a Truce, and a Ring, and a table where winner and loser eat side by side."], ["h", 2, "Dawn at the Ring"], ["p", "Every morning, before the Ring opens, the Arbiter sweeps it. He does not have to; there are a dozen people at the Ringside who would. He sweeps the sand in the straw-rope circle where a boy he did not like for the first hour once threw salt, and his father, who lays the Ringside's paving now, tells him his lines are crooked. Then the Tower's shadow comes round the Dial from the north, as the sun comes up over the morning cliffs, and lies for a moment across the circle, the way it lay there for a hundred years, and then moves on."], ["p", "The Hourkeepers write the hour down. The bells of Aubade ring a hundred. The gate opens for the first champion of the day."], ["p", "The Long Noon is over. The day has begun."]]}};
